WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.952 align:middle line:90%
[SQUEAKING]

00:00:01.952 --> 00:00:03.416 align:middle line:90%
[RUSTLING]

00:00:03.416 --> 00:00:06.344 align:middle line:90%
[CLICKING]

00:00:06.344 --> 00:00:11.780 align:middle line:90%


00:00:11.780 --> 00:00:13.100 align:middle line:90%
[SIDE CONVERSATIONS]

00:00:13.100 --> 00:00:16.260 align:middle line:90%


00:00:16.260 --> 00:00:19.550 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: OK,
so let's start back up.

00:00:19.550 --> 00:00:20.630 align:middle line:90%
Today, let's see.

00:00:20.630 --> 00:00:24.240 align:middle line:84%
We are transitioning from
morphology into phonetics.

00:00:24.240 --> 00:00:26.060 align:middle line:84%
So I hope you
enjoyed morphology.

00:00:26.060 --> 00:00:28.490 align:middle line:84%
It's not as if you won't
ever do any morphology again.

00:00:28.490 --> 00:00:33.170 align:middle line:84%
But that's it for lessons on
morphology-- lectures, I guess.

00:00:33.170 --> 00:00:36.140 align:middle line:84%
Today is phonetics, which means
that today we begin making

00:00:36.140 --> 00:00:37.800 align:middle line:90%
funny sounds at each other.

00:00:37.800 --> 00:00:42.060 align:middle line:84%
So everybody limber
up your vocal tracts.

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:42.560 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:00:42.560 --> 00:00:44.330 align:middle line:84%
I'm trying to remember
if there's anything

00:00:44.330 --> 00:00:46.100 align:middle line:90%
that I ought to announce.

00:00:46.100 --> 00:00:47.990 align:middle line:84%
You remember,
maybe, that problem

00:00:47.990 --> 00:00:51.080 align:middle line:84%
set 1, which confusingly
is your second problem set,

00:00:51.080 --> 00:00:53.030 align:middle line:90%
is due on Thursday.

00:00:53.030 --> 00:00:54.620 align:middle line:84%
Normally, it would
be due on Tuesday.

00:00:54.620 --> 00:00:58.430 align:middle line:84%
But because I am
technologically challenged,

00:00:58.430 --> 00:01:00.975 align:middle line:90%
it's due on Thursday.

00:01:00.975 --> 00:01:02.850 align:middle line:84%
Speaking of being
technologically challenged,

00:01:02.850 --> 00:01:04.760 align:middle line:84%
I just figured out how
to get the projector

00:01:04.760 --> 00:01:06.740 align:middle line:84%
to project over there
instead of in the middle

00:01:06.740 --> 00:01:08.720 align:middle line:84%
so that I won't have to write
everything twice running

00:01:08.720 --> 00:01:09.870 align:middle line:90%
back and forth across the room.

00:01:09.870 --> 00:01:10.160 align:middle line:90%
[APPLAUSE]

00:01:10.160 --> 00:01:10.640 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:01:10.640 --> 00:01:11.160 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:01:11.160 --> 00:01:11.660 align:middle line:90%
Thank you.

00:01:11.660 --> 00:01:14.690 align:middle line:90%


00:01:14.690 --> 00:01:16.760 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to mention
this when I go up for--

00:01:16.760 --> 00:01:19.160 align:middle line:84%
my chair is trying to decide
whether to give me a raise.

00:01:19.160 --> 00:01:21.260 align:middle line:84%
I'll be like, big academic
achievement of the year

00:01:21.260 --> 00:01:24.030 align:middle line:90%
was figuring out the technique.

00:01:24.030 --> 00:01:25.753 align:middle line:84%
So if anybody
misses the old days,

00:01:25.753 --> 00:01:27.920 align:middle line:84%
like you were amused watching
me run back and forth,

00:01:27.920 --> 00:01:32.030 align:middle line:84%
or this turns out to be too
small or something like that,

00:01:32.030 --> 00:01:32.540 align:middle line:90%
let me know.

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:36.580 align:middle line:84%
And we'll go back
to the old days.

00:01:36.580 --> 00:01:37.790 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:01:37.790 --> 00:01:43.370 align:middle line:84%
So when we speak, if we're
speaking an oral language,

00:01:43.370 --> 00:01:47.390 align:middle line:84%
if we're not signing, what
we are typically doing

00:01:47.390 --> 00:01:50.390 align:middle line:84%
is producing a flow of air,
which typically comes out

00:01:50.390 --> 00:01:52.040 align:middle line:84%
of your lungs, but
not necessarily.

00:01:52.040 --> 00:01:53.900 align:middle line:90%
We'll talk about that.

00:01:53.900 --> 00:01:57.170 align:middle line:84%
And it gets obstructed
in various ways

00:01:57.170 --> 00:01:58.770 align:middle line:90%
in the vocal tract.

00:01:58.770 --> 00:02:01.160 align:middle line:84%
And so one standard
way of talking

00:02:01.160 --> 00:02:02.990 align:middle line:84%
about different kinds
of speech sounds

00:02:02.990 --> 00:02:08.150 align:middle line:84%
is to talk about where in the
vocal tract the flow of air

00:02:08.150 --> 00:02:09.860 align:middle line:90%
can get obstructed and how.

00:02:09.860 --> 00:02:12.510 align:middle line:84%
And that's what
we're going to do.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:14.000 align:middle line:84%
So first, we'll
talk about where.

00:02:14.000 --> 00:02:17.030 align:middle line:84%
So one way of categorizing
the various things

00:02:17.030 --> 00:02:20.870 align:middle line:84%
that your vocal tract
does to the airflow

00:02:20.870 --> 00:02:22.880 align:middle line:84%
is by what's called
place of articulation.

00:02:22.880 --> 00:02:25.880 align:middle line:84%
That is, where in your vocal
tract is the flow of air

00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.200 align:middle line:90%
getting obstructed?

00:02:28.200 --> 00:02:32.490 align:middle line:84%
So for example, there are what
are called bilabial sounds.

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:35.360 align:middle line:84%
These are sounds which
are made with both lips.

00:02:35.360 --> 00:02:37.040 align:middle line:84%
This picture over
here on the right

00:02:37.040 --> 00:02:38.720 align:middle line:84%
is what's called the
sagittal section.

00:02:38.720 --> 00:02:40.790 align:middle line:84%
That is, it's a picture
of someone's head cut

00:02:40.790 --> 00:02:46.190 align:middle line:84%
in half so that you can see
the stuff that's inside.

00:02:46.190 --> 00:02:48.680 align:middle line:84%
And those arrows
are meant to get

00:02:48.680 --> 00:02:52.040 align:middle line:84%
you to imagine that this person
is making a sound by putting

00:02:52.040 --> 00:02:53.280 align:middle line:90%
their two lips together.

00:02:53.280 --> 00:02:55.070 align:middle line:84%
So that's what you do
for the sounds that

00:02:55.070 --> 00:02:58.310 align:middle line:84%
are at the beginnings of words
like "paint," and "bath,"

00:02:58.310 --> 00:03:02.220 align:middle line:84%
and "mouth," and well, "wipe,"
where your lips don't touch,

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:03.440 align:middle line:90%
but they both move.

00:03:03.440 --> 00:03:04.370 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:03:04.370 --> 00:03:06.800 align:middle line:84%
Everybody feel free to
confirm to yourselves

00:03:06.800 --> 00:03:10.130 align:middle line:84%
in the privacy of your own mask
that that is what you're doing

00:03:10.130 --> 00:03:11.690 align:middle line:90%
when you make these sounds.

00:03:11.690 --> 00:03:14.900 align:middle line:90%
Now next to the sounds--

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:17.480 align:middle line:84%
so I have these words-- "paint,"
"bath," "map," and "wipe."

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:21.617 align:middle line:84%
And then next to them, I have
these symbols in brackets.

00:03:21.617 --> 00:03:24.200 align:middle line:84%
And they may not look at it, but
these are extremely technical

00:03:24.200 --> 00:03:24.720 align:middle line:90%
symbols.

00:03:24.720 --> 00:03:26.840 align:middle line:84%
These are symbols of the
International Phonetic

00:03:26.840 --> 00:03:27.830 align:middle line:90%
Alphabet.

00:03:27.830 --> 00:03:31.580 align:middle line:84%
So linguists have a system
for writing sounds down so

00:03:31.580 --> 00:03:33.320 align:middle line:84%
that we'll all know
what kind of sound

00:03:33.320 --> 00:03:36.735 align:middle line:84%
we're talking about when
we're talking about sounds.

00:03:36.735 --> 00:03:37.235 align:middle line:90%
And--

00:03:37.235 --> 00:03:38.030 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [SNEEZES]

00:03:38.030 --> 00:03:38.750 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Bless you.

00:03:38.750 --> 00:03:41.450 align:middle line:84%
A lot of the symbols of the
International Phonetic Alphabet

00:03:41.450 --> 00:03:43.340 align:middle line:84%
resemble letters of
the English alphabet.

00:03:43.340 --> 00:03:45.080 align:middle line:84%
So we have started
here with symbols

00:03:45.080 --> 00:03:46.530 align:middle line:90%
that should all look familiar.

00:03:46.530 --> 00:03:50.090 align:middle line:84%
So the symbol for the sound
at the beginning of "paint"

00:03:50.090 --> 00:03:51.420 align:middle line:90%
is the letter p.

00:03:51.420 --> 00:03:51.920 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:03:51.920 --> 00:03:54.770 align:middle line:84%
And so that's a symbol of
the International Phonetic

00:03:54.770 --> 00:03:55.310 align:middle line:90%
Alphabet.

00:03:55.310 --> 00:03:57.440 align:middle line:84%
As we go along, we will be
seeing weirder and weirder

00:03:57.440 --> 00:03:59.482 align:middle line:84%
symbols from the International
Phonetic Alphabet.

00:03:59.482 --> 00:04:00.960 align:middle line:84%
So please enjoy it
while it's easy.

00:04:00.960 --> 00:04:01.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:04:01.460 --> 00:04:02.780 align:middle line:90%
So far, so good.

00:04:02.780 --> 00:04:03.280 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:04:03.280 --> 00:04:07.260 align:middle line:90%


00:04:07.260 --> 00:04:10.110 align:middle line:84%
There are also what are
called labiodental sounds.

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:14.160 align:middle line:84%
Labiodental sounds involve your
top teeth and your lower lip.

00:04:14.160 --> 00:04:15.660 align:middle line:84%
If you think about
what you're doing

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:20.160 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning of a sound
like "face" or "vase,"

00:04:20.160 --> 00:04:22.410 align:middle line:84%
what you're doing, at
least if you're me,

00:04:22.410 --> 00:04:25.170 align:middle line:84%
is bringing your
lower lip pretty close

00:04:25.170 --> 00:04:28.080 align:middle line:84%
to your upper teeth and
obstructing the flow of air

00:04:28.080 --> 00:04:30.120 align:middle line:84%
there so that there's
some turbulence.

00:04:30.120 --> 00:04:31.410 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:04:31.410 --> 00:04:34.590 align:middle line:84%
There are, I
believe, no languages

00:04:34.590 --> 00:04:37.320 align:middle line:84%
in which labiodentals are
made with your bottom teeth

00:04:37.320 --> 00:04:38.490 align:middle line:90%
and your upper lip.

00:04:38.490 --> 00:04:41.920 align:middle line:84%
It's a little hard to
do-- "face," "face."

00:04:41.920 --> 00:04:44.478 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about other kinds
of places of articulation

00:04:44.478 --> 00:04:46.020 align:middle line:84%
that English doesn't
use, but I think

00:04:46.020 --> 00:04:48.380 align:middle line:90%
that one just doesn't exist.

00:04:48.380 --> 00:04:50.933 align:middle line:84%
And again, two symbols
of the IPA, which again,

00:04:50.933 --> 00:04:52.850 align:middle line:84%
are not going to be too
hard for you to learn.

00:04:52.850 --> 00:04:55.430 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so the symbol for
the sound of the beginning

00:04:55.430 --> 00:04:56.375 align:middle line:90%
of "face" is an f.

00:04:56.375 --> 00:04:58.667 align:middle line:84%
And the symbol for the sound
of the beginning of "vase"

00:04:58.667 --> 00:05:02.240 align:middle line:84%
is a v. Here we get
our first two IPA

00:05:02.240 --> 00:05:05.870 align:middle line:84%
symbols that are not letters
of the English alphabet.

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:08.330 align:middle line:90%
These are interdental sounds.

00:05:08.330 --> 00:05:10.370 align:middle line:84%
Interdental sounds
are linguistically not

00:05:10.370 --> 00:05:12.140 align:middle line:90%
hugely common.

00:05:12.140 --> 00:05:13.940 align:middle line:90%
We have them in English.

00:05:13.940 --> 00:05:16.880 align:middle line:84%
There are various dialects
of Arabic that have them.

00:05:16.880 --> 00:05:19.280 align:middle line:84%
Some of the Berber
languages have them.

00:05:19.280 --> 00:05:23.127 align:middle line:90%
But they're not all that common.

00:05:23.127 --> 00:05:24.710 align:middle line:84%
These are the sounds
of the beginnings

00:05:24.710 --> 00:05:27.860 align:middle line:84%
of words like
"thistle" and "this,"

00:05:27.860 --> 00:05:30.710 align:middle line:84%
where you're sticking your
tongue between your front teeth

00:05:30.710 --> 00:05:33.320 align:middle line:90%
and making air flow out.

00:05:33.320 --> 00:05:33.820 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:05:33.820 --> 00:05:35.237 align:middle line:84%
"Th" [pronounced
as in "thistle"],

00:05:35.237 --> 00:05:36.770 align:middle line:90%
"th" [pronounced as in "this"].

00:05:36.770 --> 00:05:39.980 align:middle line:84%
English doesn't spell
these differently,

00:05:39.980 --> 00:05:41.450 align:middle line:90%
at least not reliably, right?

00:05:41.450 --> 00:05:43.273 align:middle line:84%
So we spell both of
these with a "th."

00:05:43.273 --> 00:05:45.440 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the reasons
the International Phonetic

00:05:45.440 --> 00:05:46.767 align:middle line:90%
Alphabet is there.

00:05:46.767 --> 00:05:48.350 align:middle line:84%
It's so that we can
unambiguously talk

00:05:48.350 --> 00:05:49.700 align:middle line:90%
about what we're talking about.

00:05:49.700 --> 00:05:52.370 align:middle line:84%
So the sound of the beginning
of "thistle" and the sound

00:05:52.370 --> 00:05:54.020 align:middle line:90%
of the beginning of "this"--

00:05:54.020 --> 00:05:55.220 align:middle line:90%
they're not the same sound.

00:05:55.220 --> 00:05:57.630 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk about the difference
between them shortly.

00:05:57.630 --> 00:05:59.810 align:middle line:84%
But at this point, maybe
it's just clear they're not

00:05:59.810 --> 00:06:00.480 align:middle line:90%
the same sound.

00:06:00.480 --> 00:06:02.570 align:middle line:84%
So there are two different
IPA symbols for them.

00:06:02.570 --> 00:06:04.490 align:middle line:84%
The Greek letter theta
is used for the sound

00:06:04.490 --> 00:06:06.050 align:middle line:90%
at the beginning of "thistle."

00:06:06.050 --> 00:06:09.162 align:middle line:84%
And that second letter
is an old English letter.

00:06:09.162 --> 00:06:10.370 align:middle line:90%
It's still used in Icelandic.

00:06:10.370 --> 00:06:12.890 align:middle line:84%
It's sometimes
called "edh," and it

00:06:12.890 --> 00:06:15.410 align:middle line:84%
stands for the sound at
the beginning of "this."

00:06:15.410 --> 00:06:17.270 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:17.770 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:06:17.770 --> 00:06:20.940 align:middle line:90%


00:06:20.940 --> 00:06:25.000 align:middle line:84%
Then there are what are
called alveolar sounds.

00:06:25.000 --> 00:06:27.770 align:middle line:90%
If you put your tongue--

00:06:27.770 --> 00:06:29.020 align:middle line:90%
sorry, let me just talk first.

00:06:29.020 --> 00:06:32.890 align:middle line:84%
If you put your tongue at the
top of your mouth and drag

00:06:32.890 --> 00:06:35.410 align:middle line:84%
it-- so put it first
against your front teeth

00:06:35.410 --> 00:06:39.670 align:middle line:84%
and then drag it backwards
along the top of your mouth.

00:06:39.670 --> 00:06:44.200 align:middle line:84%
If you're like me,
you've got your teeth.

00:06:44.200 --> 00:06:47.170 align:middle line:84%
And then there's
this flat-ish space.

00:06:47.170 --> 00:06:49.250 align:middle line:90%
And then it goes up, yeah?

00:06:49.250 --> 00:06:52.722 align:middle line:84%
So from your teeth, you go
back to this little plateau.

00:06:52.722 --> 00:06:55.180 align:middle line:84%
There is this little plateau
there right behind your teeth,

00:06:55.180 --> 00:06:56.050 align:middle line:90%
this gum ridge.

00:06:56.050 --> 00:06:59.530 align:middle line:84%
And then your mouth begins
to go up higher, yeah?

00:06:59.530 --> 00:07:02.860 align:middle line:84%
That ridge is called
the alveolar ridge.

00:07:02.860 --> 00:07:05.510 align:middle line:84%
And if you put
your tongue there,

00:07:05.510 --> 00:07:07.510 align:middle line:84%
then you are all
set to make sounds

00:07:07.510 --> 00:07:12.680 align:middle line:84%
like the sound at the beginning
of the word "teeth," or "duck,"

00:07:12.680 --> 00:07:14.180 align:middle line:90%
or "nail."

00:07:14.180 --> 00:07:17.188 align:middle line:84%
And for sounds like
"sail" and "zoom," again,

00:07:17.188 --> 00:07:18.980 align:middle line:84%
you're not actually
touching that position.

00:07:18.980 --> 00:07:21.380 align:middle line:84%
We'll talk more about the
difference between "s" and "t"

00:07:21.380 --> 00:07:22.290 align:middle line:90%
in just a second.

00:07:22.290 --> 00:07:23.840 align:middle line:84%
But they're both
alveolar sounds.

00:07:23.840 --> 00:07:25.910 align:middle line:84%
Your tongue is pointing
in the general direction

00:07:25.910 --> 00:07:28.190 align:middle line:90%
of your alveolar ridge.

00:07:28.190 --> 00:07:29.630 align:middle line:90%
Everybody have that sensation?

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:32.690 align:middle line:84%
You should all be exploring
the insides of your own heads

00:07:32.690 --> 00:07:35.215 align:middle line:90%
right now, yeah?

00:07:35.215 --> 00:07:35.715 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:07:35.715 --> 00:07:41.540 align:middle line:90%


00:07:41.540 --> 00:07:44.450 align:middle line:84%
There's a type of sound that
has been called alveopalatal.

00:07:44.450 --> 00:07:45.800 align:middle line:90%
It's also called postalveolar.

00:07:45.800 --> 00:07:47.960 align:middle line:90%
I'll call it postalveolar.

00:07:47.960 --> 00:07:51.830 align:middle line:84%
If you think first about an
"s" and then you think about

00:07:51.830 --> 00:07:55.100 align:middle line:84%
a "sh," the "sh" at the
beginning of "ship,"

00:07:55.100 --> 00:08:01.620 align:middle line:84%
if you go back and forth
between them-- s, sh, s, sh--

00:08:01.620 --> 00:08:04.030 align:middle line:90%
you make a very soothing sound.

00:08:04.030 --> 00:08:04.530 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:08:04.530 --> 00:08:06.930 align:middle line:84%
And maybe what you can
feel is that your tongue

00:08:06.930 --> 00:08:08.972 align:middle line:90%
is rocking back and forth.

00:08:08.972 --> 00:08:10.680 align:middle line:84%
At least, that's what
my tongue is doing.

00:08:10.680 --> 00:08:15.030 align:middle line:84%
For "s," it's pointing at the
front at my alveolar ridge.

00:08:15.030 --> 00:08:18.960 align:middle line:84%
And then for "sh," the
middle of my tongue curls up

00:08:18.960 --> 00:08:21.668 align:middle line:84%
a little bit, goes a
little further back.

00:08:21.668 --> 00:08:23.210 align:middle line:84%
Is that the feeling
people are having

00:08:23.210 --> 00:08:25.127 align:middle line:84%
as they're going back
and forth between these?

00:08:25.127 --> 00:08:27.077 align:middle line:90%


00:08:27.077 --> 00:08:28.160 align:middle line:90%
Here's another new symbol.

00:08:28.160 --> 00:08:30.770 align:middle line:84%
That's the symbol for the sound
of the beginning of a "ship,"

00:08:30.770 --> 00:08:31.790 align:middle line:90%
and another new symbol.

00:08:31.790 --> 00:08:37.520 align:middle line:84%
That's the sound in the middle
of "azure," the "zh" sound.

00:08:37.520 --> 00:08:39.740 align:middle line:84%
Both of those are
postalveolar sounds.

00:08:39.740 --> 00:08:42.230 align:middle line:84%
You'll sometimes see them
called alveopalatal sounds.

00:08:42.230 --> 00:08:45.231 align:middle line:84%
I'll try to remember to
always call them postalveolar.

00:08:45.231 --> 00:08:45.731 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:08:45.731 --> 00:08:48.480 align:middle line:90%


00:08:48.480 --> 00:08:53.527 align:middle line:84%
A little further back, there are
what are called palatal sounds.

00:08:53.527 --> 00:08:55.860 align:middle line:84%
These are either even further
behind the alveolar ridge,

00:08:55.860 --> 00:08:57.930 align:middle line:84%
back where the
roof of your mouth

00:08:57.930 --> 00:08:59.520 align:middle line:84%
gets as high as
it's going to get.

00:08:59.520 --> 00:09:02.280 align:middle line:84%
And the one palatal sound
that we have in English

00:09:02.280 --> 00:09:04.920 align:middle line:84%
is the "y" sound at the
beginning of "year," yeah?

00:09:04.920 --> 00:09:07.410 align:middle line:84%
And here's the first IPA
symbol that is deliberately

00:09:07.410 --> 00:09:09.240 align:middle line:90%
designed to confuse you.

00:09:09.240 --> 00:09:11.580 align:middle line:84%
The IPA symbol for
that sound, the "y"

00:09:11.580 --> 00:09:14.070 align:middle line:84%
at the beginning of
"year," is a "j," yeah.

00:09:14.070 --> 00:09:18.390 align:middle line:84%
This is because the IPA was
not invented exclusively

00:09:18.390 --> 00:09:19.830 align:middle line:90%
by speakers of English, right?

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:22.320 align:middle line:84%
So it was invented also
by speakers of languages

00:09:22.320 --> 00:09:26.610 align:middle line:84%
like German, in which they use
this letter for this sound.

00:09:26.610 --> 00:09:28.710 align:middle line:84%
"Ja," for example,
the German word

00:09:28.710 --> 00:09:30.810 align:middle line:90%
for "yes," spelled J-A, yeah?

00:09:30.810 --> 00:09:33.240 align:middle line:90%
Great.

00:09:33.240 --> 00:09:35.140 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:09:35.140 --> 00:09:36.940 align:middle line:84%
The letter y is used
for something else.

00:09:36.940 --> 00:09:39.470 align:middle line:90%
We'll get to it.

00:09:39.470 --> 00:09:40.100 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:09:40.100 --> 00:09:42.852 align:middle line:84%
And then continuing
our tour of the mouth,

00:09:42.852 --> 00:09:45.060 align:middle line:84%
so we're working our way
backwards through the mouth,

00:09:45.060 --> 00:09:47.060 align:middle line:84%
there are what are
called velar sounds.

00:09:47.060 --> 00:09:50.150 align:middle line:84%
In the velar sound,
the body of your tongue

00:09:50.150 --> 00:09:52.067 align:middle line:84%
is up against what's
called the velum, which

00:09:52.067 --> 00:09:53.900 align:middle line:84%
is the soft tissue at
the back of your mouth

00:09:53.900 --> 00:09:56.150 align:middle line:84%
that we're going to be hearing
more about in a second.

00:09:56.150 --> 00:09:58.280 align:middle line:84%
It's responsible for
partitioning your mouth

00:09:58.280 --> 00:10:02.280 align:middle line:84%
from your nose through
your oral cavity.

00:10:02.280 --> 00:10:05.630 align:middle line:84%
So that's the place that
your tongue is touching

00:10:05.630 --> 00:10:07.820 align:middle line:84%
when you say the sounds
at the beginnings of words

00:10:07.820 --> 00:10:12.270 align:middle line:84%
like "kernel," and
"caught," and "gone,"

00:10:12.270 --> 00:10:14.540 align:middle line:84%
and when you make the
nasal sound at the end

00:10:14.540 --> 00:10:16.520 align:middle line:90%
of a word like "sing."

00:10:16.520 --> 00:10:21.500 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about where your
tongue is, it's up in the back

00:10:21.500 --> 00:10:22.280 align:middle line:90%
there.

00:10:22.280 --> 00:10:24.710 align:middle line:90%
Does that sound right?

00:10:24.710 --> 00:10:27.180 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:10:27.180 --> 00:10:30.590 align:middle line:90%
OK, cool.

00:10:30.590 --> 00:10:35.060 align:middle line:84%
And then going even
further down, further down

00:10:35.060 --> 00:10:38.480 align:middle line:84%
in your throat, you've got
the vocal cords, the glottis,

00:10:38.480 --> 00:10:42.530 align:middle line:84%
this space that's down there in
your throat around your larynx,

00:10:42.530 --> 00:10:45.500 align:middle line:90%
and your vocal cords.

00:10:45.500 --> 00:10:47.660 align:middle line:84%
Or they're sometimes
called your vocal folds.

00:10:47.660 --> 00:10:49.970 align:middle line:84%
This process that you've
got back here in your throat

00:10:49.970 --> 00:10:53.570 align:middle line:84%
for closing off the
air can be closed

00:10:53.570 --> 00:10:58.070 align:middle line:84%
to make what's called
a glottal stop.

00:10:58.070 --> 00:11:02.000 align:middle line:84%
English doesn't
make a huge amount

00:11:02.000 --> 00:11:03.470 align:middle line:90%
of use of the glottal stop.

00:11:03.470 --> 00:11:07.470 align:middle line:84%
But it's what shows up at
the beginnings of words like

00:11:07.470 --> 00:11:10.490 align:middle line:84%
"uh-uh," that catch that
you're getting in your throat.

00:11:10.490 --> 00:11:13.010 align:middle line:90%
"Uh-uh," meaning "no," right?

00:11:13.010 --> 00:11:17.450 align:middle line:90%
Or "uh-oh," meaning "oh, dear."

00:11:17.450 --> 00:11:18.170 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:11:18.170 --> 00:11:20.420 align:middle line:84%
That catch that you're getting
in your throat-- that's

00:11:20.420 --> 00:11:21.470 align:middle line:90%
a glottal stop, yeah?

00:11:21.470 --> 00:11:23.870 align:middle line:84%
There are languages that
are into glottal stops that

00:11:23.870 --> 00:11:25.070 align:middle line:90%
have lots of them.

00:11:25.070 --> 00:11:27.560 align:middle line:90%
English is not.

00:11:27.560 --> 00:11:30.423 align:middle line:84%
And the way you make
that is by basically

00:11:30.423 --> 00:11:31.840 align:middle line:84%
slamming your vocal
folds together

00:11:31.840 --> 00:11:34.000 align:middle line:90%
to close off the flow of air.

00:11:34.000 --> 00:11:36.280 align:middle line:84%
You can also hold
them close together

00:11:36.280 --> 00:11:38.140 align:middle line:90%
and let the air whistle past.

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:39.850 align:middle line:84%
That's how you
make an "h," right?

00:11:39.850 --> 00:11:42.460 align:middle line:84%
As in, what's my word
up there-- "help," yeah.

00:11:42.460 --> 00:11:45.370 align:middle line:84%
So you're just slightly
abrading the flow of air.

00:11:45.370 --> 00:11:47.790 align:middle line:90%


00:11:47.790 --> 00:11:48.290 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:11:48.290 --> 00:11:51.457 align:middle line:90%


00:11:51.457 --> 00:11:53.290 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, there are questions
about any of that?

00:11:53.290 --> 00:11:54.950 align:middle line:84%
So that was just a quick
tour through the vocal tract.

00:11:54.950 --> 00:11:55.640 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:11:55.640 --> 00:11:57.418 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Where's, like, "chuh"?

00:11:57.418 --> 00:11:59.460 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, we
haven't gotten to "chuh."

00:11:59.460 --> 00:12:01.240 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, but good question.

00:12:01.240 --> 00:12:04.770 align:middle line:90%
We can think of what that is.

00:12:04.770 --> 00:12:06.840 align:middle line:84%
A "ch" sound at the
beginning of a word

00:12:06.840 --> 00:12:12.840 align:middle line:84%
like "church," also at the
end of a word like "church,"

00:12:12.840 --> 00:12:15.960 align:middle line:90%
is a dynamic sound, right?

00:12:15.960 --> 00:12:20.670 align:middle line:84%
Ch-- I think your
tongue is in motion

00:12:20.670 --> 00:12:22.330 align:middle line:90%
as you are making that sound.

00:12:22.330 --> 00:12:22.830 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:12:22.830 --> 00:12:26.790 align:middle line:90%
So it makes one--

00:12:26.790 --> 00:12:29.100 align:middle line:84%
it completely stops
the flow of air.

00:12:29.100 --> 00:12:33.540 align:middle line:84%
And then it gradually peels back
and allows the air to flow out.

00:12:33.540 --> 00:12:36.750 align:middle line:84%
You can think of that
first thing it's doing as--

00:12:36.750 --> 00:12:39.180 align:middle line:84%
I'm on completely
the wrong slide--

00:12:39.180 --> 00:12:42.210 align:middle line:84%
as being like an
alveolar stop, right?

00:12:42.210 --> 00:12:44.820 align:middle line:90%
So it's like a "t."

00:12:44.820 --> 00:12:48.930 align:middle line:84%
And then as it peels
back, "ch," you

00:12:48.930 --> 00:12:52.020 align:middle line:84%
end up with something like a
"shuh," like a postalveolar.

00:12:52.020 --> 00:12:54.530 align:middle line:84%
So it's a pretty
complicated sound.

00:12:54.530 --> 00:12:55.530 align:middle line:90%
And we'll talk about it.

00:12:55.530 --> 00:12:58.670 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, that's a
really good question.

00:12:58.670 --> 00:12:59.350 align:middle line:90%
Other questions?

00:12:59.350 --> 00:12:59.980 align:middle line:90%
Somebody had a question?

00:12:59.980 --> 00:13:00.270 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:13:00.270 --> 00:13:02.580 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: What's the difference
between a glottal stop

00:13:02.580 --> 00:13:03.970 align:middle line:90%
and an ending consonant?

00:13:03.970 --> 00:13:04.360 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: I'm sorry.

00:13:04.360 --> 00:13:04.930 align:middle line:90%
Say it again?

00:13:04.930 --> 00:13:07.513 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: What's the difference
between a glottal stop and and,

00:13:07.513 --> 00:13:08.953 align:middle line:90%
say, an ending consonant?

00:13:08.953 --> 00:13:10.495 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: An
ending consonant?

00:13:10.495 --> 00:13:11.197 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Yeah.

00:13:11.197 --> 00:13:13.530 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: What kind
of ending consonant did you--

00:13:13.530 --> 00:13:14.763 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

00:13:14.763 --> 00:13:15.930 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, oh, oh.

00:13:15.930 --> 00:13:17.590 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, so that's a
really good point.

00:13:17.590 --> 00:13:24.000 align:middle line:84%
So glottal stop-- take
a word like put, right?

00:13:24.000 --> 00:13:27.090 align:middle line:90%


00:13:27.090 --> 00:13:30.600 align:middle line:90%
If you say put, your tongue--

00:13:30.600 --> 00:13:32.130 align:middle line:90%
at least, my tongue--

00:13:32.130 --> 00:13:33.420 align:middle line:90%
touches the alveolar ridge.

00:13:33.420 --> 00:13:36.210 align:middle line:84%
It goes where I
said it would, yeah?

00:13:36.210 --> 00:13:37.530 align:middle line:90%
But you're right.

00:13:37.530 --> 00:13:40.530 align:middle line:84%
You also make a glottal stop,
at least the way I just said it.

00:13:40.530 --> 00:13:42.112 align:middle line:90%
Put, yeah?

00:13:42.112 --> 00:13:46.770 align:middle line:90%


00:13:46.770 --> 00:13:48.040 align:middle line:90%
You could contrast that.

00:13:48.040 --> 00:13:50.700 align:middle line:84%
If you didn't make the
closure at the alveolar ridge,

00:13:50.700 --> 00:13:53.470 align:middle line:90%
it would sound like "puh."

00:13:53.470 --> 00:13:54.790 align:middle line:90%
"Puh," right?

00:13:54.790 --> 00:13:56.890 align:middle line:84%
Which is not an
English word for me.

00:13:56.890 --> 00:13:59.530 align:middle line:84%
There are dialects of
English in which that's

00:13:59.530 --> 00:14:01.430 align:middle line:90%
something you would say, right?

00:14:01.430 --> 00:14:03.640 align:middle line:90%
"Puh."

00:14:03.640 --> 00:14:07.570 align:middle line:84%
There are places
in English where

00:14:07.570 --> 00:14:11.290 align:middle line:84%
things that we write as
other kinds of sounds

00:14:11.290 --> 00:14:13.840 align:middle line:84%
actually are, in fact, glottal
stops, at least in my English.

00:14:13.840 --> 00:14:17.065 align:middle line:84%
So the difference for me
between "can" and "can't"--

00:14:17.065 --> 00:14:19.840 align:middle line:90%


00:14:19.840 --> 00:14:22.060 align:middle line:84%
my wife, who is
Japanese, is driven crazy

00:14:22.060 --> 00:14:24.970 align:middle line:84%
by the difference between "can"
and "can't" because they're

00:14:24.970 --> 00:14:27.310 align:middle line:90%
virtually the same, right?

00:14:27.310 --> 00:14:29.260 align:middle line:84%
It's very hard for her
to figure out, often,

00:14:29.260 --> 00:14:31.000 align:middle line:84%
whether I'm saying
"can" or "can't."

00:14:31.000 --> 00:14:34.840 align:middle line:84%
Because the difference between
them is really mostly just--

00:14:34.840 --> 00:14:38.980 align:middle line:84%
"can't" is really just
"can" plus a glottal stop.

00:14:38.980 --> 00:14:42.520 align:middle line:84%
I'm not saying "can't," usually,
unless I'm being very emphatic.

00:14:42.520 --> 00:14:44.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good question.

00:14:44.950 --> 00:14:46.900 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:14:46.900 --> 00:14:49.150 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Is it cool
for the same sound

00:14:49.150 --> 00:14:54.400 align:middle line:84%
to be heard audibly but with a
different part of your mouth.

00:14:54.400 --> 00:14:56.660 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes, yes,
there are things like that.

00:14:56.660 --> 00:14:57.910 align:middle line:90%
Do you have something in mind?

00:14:57.910 --> 00:14:58.190 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: No.

00:14:58.190 --> 00:14:59.290 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, OK.

00:14:59.290 --> 00:15:01.780 align:middle line:90%
So we haven't gotten yet to "r."

00:15:01.780 --> 00:15:03.250 align:middle line:90%
We'll get to "r," eventually.

00:15:03.250 --> 00:15:06.200 align:middle line:84%
But actually, people
discovered at a certain point--

00:15:06.200 --> 00:15:09.202 align:middle line:84%
so people investigate this kind
of thing in all kinds of ways.

00:15:09.202 --> 00:15:11.410 align:middle line:84%
One is the kind of thing
for all doing, where we just

00:15:11.410 --> 00:15:13.060 align:middle line:84%
sit and say, what
is my mouth doing?

00:15:13.060 --> 00:15:15.970 align:middle line:90%
Hmm.

00:15:15.970 --> 00:15:19.780 align:middle line:84%
There's other kinds of work
where people classically

00:15:19.780 --> 00:15:23.450 align:middle line:84%
paint the roof of your mouth
with stuff that will come off.

00:15:23.450 --> 00:15:25.600 align:middle line:84%
And then you have
people produce a sound.

00:15:25.600 --> 00:15:27.400 align:middle line:84%
And then you stick a
camera in their mouth

00:15:27.400 --> 00:15:31.690 align:middle line:84%
and take pictures and see which
parts of the paint came off.

00:15:31.690 --> 00:15:34.510 align:middle line:84%
You stick tubes down people's
nose to measure airflow.

00:15:34.510 --> 00:15:37.480 align:middle line:84%
You do all kinds of
horrible invasive things.

00:15:37.480 --> 00:15:39.910 align:middle line:84%
These days, people
do a lot of MRIs.

00:15:39.910 --> 00:15:46.270 align:middle line:84%
I'm going to put on the website
a couple of websites that

00:15:46.270 --> 00:15:48.760 align:middle line:84%
have charts of all of
the sounds that we're

00:15:48.760 --> 00:15:52.000 align:middle line:84%
going to talk about plus
many more together with MRIs

00:15:52.000 --> 00:15:55.420 align:middle line:84%
of the insides of people's
mouths making these sounds

00:15:55.420 --> 00:15:57.527 align:middle line:84%
so that you can see the
anatomy that's involved.

00:15:57.527 --> 00:15:59.110 align:middle line:84%
You won't just have
to think about it.

00:15:59.110 --> 00:16:00.460 align:middle line:84%
One of the things
people have discovered

00:16:00.460 --> 00:16:01.877 align:middle line:84%
as they're doing
this kind of work

00:16:01.877 --> 00:16:04.150 align:middle line:84%
is that people just have
different ways of producing

00:16:04.150 --> 00:16:07.840 align:middle line:84%
"r," that there are just
different kinds of things

00:16:07.840 --> 00:16:10.390 align:middle line:84%
you can do with your anatomy
to make an "r" sound.

00:16:10.390 --> 00:16:12.730 align:middle line:84%
And that's probably
related to the fact

00:16:12.730 --> 00:16:14.820 align:middle line:84%
that "r" is one of
the kinds of sounds

00:16:14.820 --> 00:16:16.570 align:middle line:84%
that people classically
have trouble with.

00:16:16.570 --> 00:16:18.820 align:middle line:84%
If you've been around small
children, for example,

00:16:18.820 --> 00:16:22.300 align:middle line:84%
it's standard for them to
not quite get "r" right

00:16:22.300 --> 00:16:24.100 align:middle line:84%
and to say something
that sounds more

00:16:24.100 --> 00:16:27.140 align:middle line:90%
like a "w" at a certain stage.

00:16:27.140 --> 00:16:27.972 align:middle line:90%
So I'm sorry.

00:16:27.972 --> 00:16:29.680 align:middle line:84%
The short answer to
your question is yes.

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:31.015 align:middle line:90%
And "r" might be an example.

00:16:31.015 --> 00:16:33.800 align:middle line:90%


00:16:33.800 --> 00:16:34.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:16:34.340 --> 00:16:35.000 align:middle line:90%
Good questions.

00:16:35.000 --> 00:16:38.560 align:middle line:84%
Any other things we
want to talk about?

00:16:38.560 --> 00:16:41.210 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:41.210 --> 00:16:41.710 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:41.710 --> 00:16:44.010 align:middle line:90%
So we went through that.

00:16:44.010 --> 00:16:44.510 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:16:44.510 --> 00:16:46.780 align:middle line:84%
So now I already
alluded to the fact

00:16:46.780 --> 00:16:53.580 align:middle line:84%
that I had these slides that had
various places of articulation

00:16:53.580 --> 00:16:54.080 align:middle line:90%
on them.

00:16:54.080 --> 00:16:58.250 align:middle line:84%
But of course, each slide
had multiple sounds on it.

00:16:58.250 --> 00:17:00.460 align:middle line:84%
And so a place of
articulation is obviously

00:17:00.460 --> 00:17:01.875 align:middle line:90%
not the whole story.

00:17:01.875 --> 00:17:03.250 align:middle line:84%
Here's another
part of the story.

00:17:03.250 --> 00:17:05.859 align:middle line:90%
It's what's called voicing.

00:17:05.859 --> 00:17:10.119 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about
an "s" and a "z,"

00:17:10.119 --> 00:17:11.829 align:middle line:90%
those are both alveolar sounds.

00:17:11.829 --> 00:17:16.810 align:middle line:84%
Your tongue is reaching
toward the alveolar ridge.

00:17:16.810 --> 00:17:18.790 align:middle line:90%
But they're not the same.

00:17:18.790 --> 00:17:20.502 align:middle line:84%
What's the difference
between them?

00:17:20.502 --> 00:17:22.960 align:middle line:84%
If you think about-- if you go
back and forth between them,

00:17:22.960 --> 00:17:31.925 align:middle line:84%
s, z, s, z, s, z, you
can feel a buzzing.

00:17:31.925 --> 00:17:33.230 align:middle line:90%
[CHUCKLING]

00:17:33.230 --> 00:17:36.140 align:middle line:84%
And if you put your
hand right here--

00:17:36.140 --> 00:17:40.160 align:middle line:84%
sorry, if you put your
hand right here, right

00:17:40.160 --> 00:17:45.190 align:middle line:84%
here on your throat, then
when you make the "z" sound,

00:17:45.190 --> 00:17:48.280 align:middle line:84%
you can find the
source of that buzzing.

00:17:48.280 --> 00:17:51.100 align:middle line:84%
It's right there in
your larynx, yeah?

00:17:51.100 --> 00:17:54.310 align:middle line:84%
What's happening when you
make a "z" is that you're

00:17:54.310 --> 00:17:59.980 align:middle line:84%
holding your vocal folds across
the flow of air in such a way

00:17:59.980 --> 00:18:02.920 align:middle line:84%
that they will flap in the
breeze as the air goes by.

00:18:02.920 --> 00:18:04.510 align:middle line:90%
They'll vibrate, yeah?

00:18:04.510 --> 00:18:08.170 align:middle line:84%
It's like whistling
with a blade of grass,

00:18:08.170 --> 00:18:10.250 align:middle line:84%
or playing a reed
instrument, right?

00:18:10.250 --> 00:18:13.870 align:middle line:84%
It's getting something
to vibrate really fast.

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:15.245 align:middle line:90%
And that's what you're hearing.

00:18:15.245 --> 00:18:17.620 align:middle line:84%
That's that buzzing sound that
you're hearing and feeling

00:18:17.620 --> 00:18:21.580 align:middle line:84%
if you put your hand right
here when you're doing a "z."

00:18:21.580 --> 00:18:26.095 align:middle line:84%
We say that "z" is voiced
and that "s" is voiceless.

00:18:26.095 --> 00:18:27.910 align:middle line:84%
So it's a distinction
in voicing.

00:18:27.910 --> 00:18:28.893 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:18:28.893 --> 00:18:32.173 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Just the
fact that [INAUDIBLE]??

00:18:32.173 --> 00:18:33.840 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah,
so the difference

00:18:33.840 --> 00:18:35.520 align:middle line:90%
between "cats" and "dogs"--

00:18:35.520 --> 00:18:36.720 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:18:36.720 --> 00:18:37.895 align:middle line:90%
So exactly.

00:18:37.895 --> 00:18:39.270 align:middle line:84%
I was going to
get to that later,

00:18:39.270 --> 00:18:40.630 align:middle line:90%
but yes, that's exactly it.

00:18:40.630 --> 00:18:44.100 align:middle line:84%
So what's the difference
between a cat and a dog?

00:18:44.100 --> 00:18:46.230 align:middle line:84%
Well, as far as a
phonologist is concerned,

00:18:46.230 --> 00:18:51.090 align:middle line:84%
the difference is that "cat"
and "dog" end in sounds

00:18:51.090 --> 00:18:53.130 align:middle line:90%
that differ in voicing.

00:18:53.130 --> 00:18:57.670 align:middle line:84%
Is "t" voiced or voiceless,
the "t" at the end of "cat"?

00:18:57.670 --> 00:18:58.720 align:middle line:90%
Voiceless, yeah.

00:18:58.720 --> 00:19:01.540 align:middle line:84%
And the "g" at the end
of "dog" is voiced.

00:19:01.540 --> 00:19:03.220 align:middle line:90%
And you're choosing "s" or "z."

00:19:03.220 --> 00:19:05.090 align:middle line:84%
You're putting the
sound that agrees

00:19:05.090 --> 00:19:07.090 align:middle line:84%
in voicing with the
consonant that's at the end.

00:19:07.090 --> 00:19:08.110 align:middle line:90%
That's exactly right.

00:19:08.110 --> 00:19:08.650 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:19:08.650 --> 00:19:14.040 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: So then is it possible
to whisper "z"s and "g"s?

00:19:14.040 --> 00:19:15.130 align:middle line:90%
Are they--

00:19:15.130 --> 00:19:16.047 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:19:16.047 --> 00:19:17.517 align:middle line:84%
So this is a really
good question

00:19:17.517 --> 00:19:19.600 align:middle line:84%
which I was hoping not to
get asked quite so soon.

00:19:19.600 --> 00:19:21.320 align:middle line:90%
But no, that's good.

00:19:21.320 --> 00:19:21.820 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:19:21.820 --> 00:19:25.845 align:middle line:84%
So she just asked, what are you
doing when you're whispering?

00:19:25.845 --> 00:19:27.220 align:middle line:84%
So if you think
about what you're

00:19:27.220 --> 00:19:30.673 align:middle line:84%
doing when you're whispering,
first of all, (WHISPERING)

00:19:30.673 --> 00:19:32.788 align:middle line:84%
your vocal cords are
not vibrating at all.

00:19:32.788 --> 00:19:34.330 align:middle line:84%
(SPEAKING NORMALLY)
Your vocal chords

00:19:34.330 --> 00:19:38.170 align:middle line:84%
are not vibrating at
all at any point, right?

00:19:38.170 --> 00:19:43.240 align:middle line:84%
So that should mean that you're
not making the distinction

00:19:43.240 --> 00:19:45.938 align:middle line:84%
between "s" and "z,"
or between "f" and "v,"

00:19:45.938 --> 00:19:47.605 align:middle line:84%
or-- what's my other
example here-- "th"

00:19:47.605 --> 00:19:48.520 align:middle line:84%
[pronounced as in "thistle"] and
"th" [pronounced as in "this"],

00:19:48.520 --> 00:19:49.020 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:19:49.020 --> 00:19:52.025 align:middle line:84%
So the difference between "bath"
and "bathe" is that the "th"

00:19:52.025 --> 00:19:54.025 align:middle line:84%
[pronounced as in "this"]
is voiced and the "th"

00:19:54.025 --> 00:19:56.620 align:middle line:84%
[pronounced as in "thistle"]
is voiceless, yeah?

00:19:56.620 --> 00:19:59.540 align:middle line:84%
But that doesn't seem
to be true, right?

00:19:59.540 --> 00:20:02.605 align:middle line:84%
If you whisper
"safe" and "save"--

00:20:02.605 --> 00:20:05.400 align:middle line:90%


00:20:05.400 --> 00:20:07.780 align:middle line:84%
(WHISPERING) "safe,"
"save"-- (SPEAKING NORMALLY)

00:20:07.780 --> 00:20:09.680 align:middle line:84%
you have the
feeling that you can

00:20:09.680 --> 00:20:11.630 align:middle line:84%
hear the difference
between them, yeah?

00:20:11.630 --> 00:20:14.060 align:middle line:84%
I think if you were to
whisper one of these--

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:15.410 align:middle line:90%
so do a controlled experiment.

00:20:15.410 --> 00:20:16.380 align:middle line:90%
Go back to your dorm.

00:20:16.380 --> 00:20:18.005 align:middle line:84%
Whisper to your dorm
mate, (WHISPERING)

00:20:18.005 --> 00:20:19.190 align:middle line:84%
"safe." (SPEAKING
NORMALLY) And then find out

00:20:19.190 --> 00:20:20.700 align:middle line:90%
what they think you said.

00:20:20.700 --> 00:20:21.200 align:middle line:90%
[CHUCKLING]

00:20:21.200 --> 00:20:25.610 align:middle line:84%
Maybe warn them in advance
of what you're going to do.

00:20:25.610 --> 00:20:29.090 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS] Oh, boy.

00:20:29.090 --> 00:20:31.800 align:middle line:90%
The complaints.

00:20:31.800 --> 00:20:32.300 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:20:32.300 --> 00:20:34.845 align:middle line:84%
So there's got to be something
else going on, right?

00:20:34.845 --> 00:20:36.470 align:middle line:84%
Let's do this experiment
again, though.

00:20:36.470 --> 00:20:38.637 align:middle line:84%
So it's easiest for you,
actually, with "f" and "v."

00:20:38.637 --> 00:20:40.080 align:middle line:90%
So let's do this thing again.

00:20:40.080 --> 00:20:42.282 align:middle line:84%
If we go back and forth
between "f" and "v"--

00:20:42.282 --> 00:20:47.630 align:middle line:84%
f, v, f, v, f, v, which
of them is voiced?

00:20:47.630 --> 00:20:48.500 align:middle line:90%
The "v," right?

00:20:48.500 --> 00:20:49.460 align:middle line:90%
The "v" is voiced.

00:20:49.460 --> 00:20:50.090 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:20:50.090 --> 00:20:52.151 align:middle line:90%
Now do it again, but whisper--

00:20:52.151 --> 00:20:55.448 align:middle line:90%
f, v, f, v, f, v, f.

00:20:55.448 --> 00:20:58.750 align:middle line:90%


00:20:58.750 --> 00:21:03.980 align:middle line:84%
Does anybody feel a difference
between "f" and "v"?

00:21:03.980 --> 00:21:06.795 align:middle line:90%
Not here, right?

00:21:06.795 --> 00:21:07.295 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:21:07.295 --> 00:21:08.756 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I guess
the "v" is more--

00:21:08.756 --> 00:21:11.440 align:middle line:90%
there's more air [INAUDIBLE]

00:21:11.440 --> 00:21:14.150 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So yes?

00:21:14.150 --> 00:21:17.340 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Also, my mouth is
opening slower, I think.

00:21:17.340 --> 00:21:20.123 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah--
f, v, f, v. Yeah, there

00:21:20.123 --> 00:21:22.290 align:middle line:84%
might be a difference in
the aperture of your mouth.

00:21:22.290 --> 00:21:23.165 align:middle line:90%
I think you're right.

00:21:23.165 --> 00:21:25.380 align:middle line:84%
And I think you're
right, too, that there's

00:21:25.380 --> 00:21:28.512 align:middle line:84%
a difference in how
fast the air is flowing.

00:21:28.512 --> 00:21:30.720 align:middle line:84%
For me, I actually have the
opposite of your feeling.

00:21:30.720 --> 00:21:34.230 align:middle line:84%
For me, f, v, f,
v-- for me, there's

00:21:34.230 --> 00:21:37.790 align:middle line:84%
more air when there's an "f"
and less air when there's a "v."

00:21:37.790 --> 00:21:38.290 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:21:38.290 --> 00:21:40.150 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I don't have it with
the "v," but when I say "v,"

00:21:40.150 --> 00:21:41.690 align:middle line:90%
my lips are a little narrower.

00:21:41.690 --> 00:21:43.447 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ooh.

00:21:43.447 --> 00:21:47.120 align:middle line:90%
F, v, f, v. Bah.

00:21:47.120 --> 00:21:48.520 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:21:48.520 --> 00:21:50.810 align:middle line:90%
My jaw is moving as I do that.

00:21:50.810 --> 00:21:53.890 align:middle line:84%
And I think that is affecting
what my lips are doing.

00:21:53.890 --> 00:21:55.360 align:middle line:90%
They're coming together more.

00:21:55.360 --> 00:21:58.540 align:middle line:84%
So I think what's happening may
be-- maybe this is an attempt

00:21:58.540 --> 00:22:03.550 align:middle line:84%
to answer your question-- is
that so I just said sounds can

00:22:03.550 --> 00:22:05.465 align:middle line:90%
be either voiced or voiceless.

00:22:05.465 --> 00:22:07.840 align:middle line:84%
If they're voiced, what it
means is that your vocal cords

00:22:07.840 --> 00:22:08.740 align:middle line:90%
are vibrating.

00:22:08.740 --> 00:22:10.540 align:middle line:84%
And I made it sound
like the way you

00:22:10.540 --> 00:22:12.610 align:middle line:84%
do that is, well, you
stick your vocal cords

00:22:12.610 --> 00:22:13.900 align:middle line:90%
into the flow of air, right?

00:22:13.900 --> 00:22:15.178 align:middle line:90%
And you make them vibrate.

00:22:15.178 --> 00:22:16.720 align:middle line:84%
But I think maybe
what we're learning

00:22:16.720 --> 00:22:18.640 align:middle line:84%
is that you do some
other things, too,

00:22:18.640 --> 00:22:21.190 align:middle line:84%
to optimize the flow
of air so that you

00:22:21.190 --> 00:22:24.820 align:middle line:84%
will get a good vibration going,
that maybe if the flow of air

00:22:24.820 --> 00:22:26.710 align:middle line:84%
is too fast and maybe
we're learning things

00:22:26.710 --> 00:22:30.160 align:middle line:84%
about the aperture of
your mouth as well,

00:22:30.160 --> 00:22:33.400 align:middle line:84%
there's a way of making sure
that the pressure that you're

00:22:33.400 --> 00:22:35.313 align:middle line:84%
getting, the rush of
air that you're getting

00:22:35.313 --> 00:22:36.730 align:middle line:84%
on your vocal cords
will make them

00:22:36.730 --> 00:22:38.200 align:middle line:90%
vibrate in just the right way.

00:22:38.200 --> 00:22:40.810 align:middle line:84%
And you're manipulating
all of that stuff

00:22:40.810 --> 00:22:42.400 align:middle line:90%
without thinking about it.

00:22:42.400 --> 00:22:44.890 align:middle line:84%
And you can still hear
it when you whisper.

00:22:44.890 --> 00:22:47.410 align:middle line:84%
So when you whisper, you're
not engaging your vocal cords,

00:22:47.410 --> 00:22:49.010 align:middle line:84%
but you're doing
all the other stuff.

00:22:49.010 --> 00:22:51.760 align:middle line:84%
And that's what you're using
to hear the difference.

00:22:51.760 --> 00:22:53.650 align:middle line:84%
There's experimental
work on this.

00:22:53.650 --> 00:22:56.370 align:middle line:84%
This is the kind of thing
people try to figure out.

00:22:56.370 --> 00:22:59.530 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, really good question.

00:22:59.530 --> 00:23:00.405 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:23:00.405 --> 00:23:02.572 align:middle line:84%
OK, so your vocal cords
can either be vibrating

00:23:02.572 --> 00:23:03.780 align:middle line:90%
or they can not be vibrating.

00:23:03.780 --> 00:23:07.560 align:middle line:84%
So you have voiced sounds and
you have voiceless sounds.

00:23:07.560 --> 00:23:10.860 align:middle line:84%
So "s" and "z" and "t"
and "d' are all alveolar.

00:23:10.860 --> 00:23:12.960 align:middle line:90%
But "s" and "t" are voiceless.

00:23:12.960 --> 00:23:15.360 align:middle line:90%
And "z" and "d" are voiced.

00:23:15.360 --> 00:23:16.530 align:middle line:90%
Does that all sound right?

00:23:16.530 --> 00:23:19.110 align:middle line:90%
Is anyone upset by any of that?

00:23:19.110 --> 00:23:19.950 align:middle line:90%
Disturbed?

00:23:19.950 --> 00:23:21.300 align:middle line:90%
Alarmed?

00:23:21.300 --> 00:23:22.930 align:middle line:90%
Hungry?

00:23:22.930 --> 00:23:23.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, anything?

00:23:23.680 --> 00:23:24.730 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:23:24.730 --> 00:23:32.370 align:middle line:84%
So it's back to
the Polish plurals.

00:23:32.370 --> 00:23:36.780 align:middle line:84%
So we saw before, we
convinced ourselves,

00:23:36.780 --> 00:23:37.950 align:middle line:90%
or I convinced myself--

00:23:37.950 --> 00:23:39.540 align:middle line:84%
and I tried to take
the rest of you

00:23:39.540 --> 00:23:41.190 align:middle line:90%
with me as collateral damage--

00:23:41.190 --> 00:23:46.110 align:middle line:84%
that Polish has words
that end in "k" and words

00:23:46.110 --> 00:23:48.210 align:middle line:90%
that end in "g" underlyingly.

00:23:48.210 --> 00:23:50.910 align:middle line:84%
But it also has a rule
that changes "g" to "k"

00:23:50.910 --> 00:23:51.960 align:middle line:90%
at the ends of words.

00:23:51.960 --> 00:23:54.590 align:middle line:90%
That was Polish, right?

00:23:54.590 --> 00:23:57.630 align:middle line:90%
OK, but it's not just "g."

00:23:57.630 --> 00:24:00.247 align:middle line:84%
So we can see some
other pairs of words.

00:24:00.247 --> 00:24:02.080 align:middle line:84%
I don't have any more
minimal pairs for you.

00:24:02.080 --> 00:24:05.940 align:middle line:84%
But you can see there's the
same general tendency that if we

00:24:05.940 --> 00:24:08.580 align:middle line:84%
look at singulars
in Polish, that they

00:24:08.580 --> 00:24:13.080 align:middle line:84%
can end in sounds like "k,"
or "b," or "t," or "s," right?

00:24:13.080 --> 00:24:15.130 align:middle line:84%
That's what we're
seeing in these pairs.

00:24:15.130 --> 00:24:20.340 align:middle line:84%
And when you pluralize them,
some nouns that end in "p"

00:24:20.340 --> 00:24:24.210 align:middle line:84%
still end in "p" when you
add the "e," the suffix,

00:24:24.210 --> 00:24:25.830 align:middle line:90%
like corpse.

00:24:25.830 --> 00:24:28.740 align:middle line:84%
But some change the
"p" to a "b," yeah?

00:24:28.740 --> 00:24:31.600 align:middle line:84%
And the same deal for
these other ones, right?

00:24:31.600 --> 00:24:36.750 align:middle line:84%
So what we're learning is it
isn't just that "g" becomes "k"

00:24:36.750 --> 00:24:39.420 align:middle line:90%
at the end of a word in Polish.

00:24:39.420 --> 00:24:41.640 align:middle line:90%
There's this more general thing.

00:24:41.640 --> 00:24:43.170 align:middle line:90%
What's the more general thing?

00:24:43.170 --> 00:24:45.280 align:middle line:90%
What's going on here?

00:24:45.280 --> 00:24:47.760 align:middle line:84%
What's the difference
between "g" and "k"?

00:24:47.760 --> 00:24:50.935 align:middle line:90%
So they're both velar.

00:24:50.935 --> 00:24:51.435 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:24:51.435 --> 00:24:52.550 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: One's
going to be voiced.

00:24:52.550 --> 00:24:53.467 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:24:53.467 --> 00:24:54.503 align:middle line:90%
Which one is voiced?

00:24:54.503 --> 00:24:55.290 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: The "g."

00:24:55.290 --> 00:24:56.582 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: The "g," yeah.

00:24:56.582 --> 00:24:59.310 align:middle line:84%
So what's happening is that
the "g," which was voiced,

00:24:59.310 --> 00:25:03.330 align:middle line:84%
is becoming voiceless at the
ends of words in Polish, yeah.

00:25:03.330 --> 00:25:07.940 align:middle line:84%
What's happening
with "b" and "p"?

00:25:07.940 --> 00:25:08.720 align:middle line:90%
Joseph?

00:25:08.720 --> 00:25:10.310 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Voiced "b"
becomes voiceless.

00:25:10.310 --> 00:25:11.390 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, the voiced "b"

00:25:11.390 --> 00:25:13.473 align:middle line:84%
becomes the voiceless
version, which is "p," yeah?

00:25:13.473 --> 00:25:15.500 align:middle line:90%
Those are both bilabial sounds.

00:25:15.500 --> 00:25:16.730 align:middle line:90%
They involve both your lips.

00:25:16.730 --> 00:25:17.795 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:25:17.795 --> 00:25:18.295 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:25:18.295 --> 00:25:19.770 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: How is "b" voiced?

00:25:19.770 --> 00:25:20.250 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Sorry?

00:25:20.250 --> 00:25:21.375 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: How is "b" voiced?

00:25:21.375 --> 00:25:23.640 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
How is "d" voiced?

00:25:23.640 --> 00:25:24.468 align:middle line:90%
Oh, "b."

00:25:24.468 --> 00:25:25.010 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Yes.

00:25:25.010 --> 00:25:25.936 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "b."

00:25:25.936 --> 00:25:26.436 align:middle line:90%
Buh.

00:25:26.436 --> 00:25:26.936 align:middle line:90%
Buh.

00:25:26.936 --> 00:25:30.100 align:middle line:90%


00:25:30.100 --> 00:25:31.310 align:middle line:90%
You're raising a good point.

00:25:31.310 --> 00:25:34.570 align:middle line:84%
There's a reason that I started
with sounds like "s" and "z,"

00:25:34.570 --> 00:25:35.680 align:middle line:90%
and "f" and "v."

00:25:35.680 --> 00:25:40.480 align:middle line:84%
Because you can go s, z, s,
z for as long as you have,

00:25:40.480 --> 00:25:41.950 align:middle line:90%
breath right?

00:25:41.950 --> 00:25:49.030 align:middle line:84%
Whereas buh-- there's a limit
to how long you can "b," yeah?

00:25:49.030 --> 00:25:51.700 align:middle line:84%
We just said the
way voicing works

00:25:51.700 --> 00:25:54.820 align:middle line:84%
is that you've got air flowing
across your vocal folds

00:25:54.820 --> 00:25:56.710 align:middle line:90%
and making them vibrate, right?

00:25:56.710 --> 00:25:59.420 align:middle line:84%
And for a "z," you can
see how that would work.

00:25:59.420 --> 00:26:00.610 align:middle line:90%
So the air just flows.

00:26:00.610 --> 00:26:06.610 align:middle line:84%
For a "b," well, the air
only has so far to go, yeah?

00:26:06.610 --> 00:26:09.610 align:middle line:84%
That's one reason you can't
keep a "b" going for very long.

00:26:09.610 --> 00:26:13.000 align:middle line:84%
All that air-- it has to
flow past your vocal folds

00:26:13.000 --> 00:26:14.500 align:middle line:84%
to get them to
vibrate a little bit.

00:26:14.500 --> 00:26:15.792 align:middle line:90%
And then it gets to your mouth.

00:26:15.792 --> 00:26:17.020 align:middle line:90%
And then it has to stop.

00:26:17.020 --> 00:26:19.390 align:middle line:84%
So there are other departments
that are better at this

00:26:19.390 --> 00:26:19.940 align:middle line:90%
than I am.

00:26:19.940 --> 00:26:21.850 align:middle line:84%
But the air pressure
in your mouth

00:26:21.850 --> 00:26:23.600 align:middle line:84%
is going to build up
past a certain point.

00:26:23.600 --> 00:26:26.080 align:middle line:84%
You won't be able
to keep doing that.

00:26:26.080 --> 00:26:28.060 align:middle line:84%
But you do it for
as long as you can.

00:26:28.060 --> 00:26:30.350 align:middle line:84%
That's the sense in
which it's voiced.

00:26:30.350 --> 00:26:32.110 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, good question.

00:26:32.110 --> 00:26:33.692 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:26:33.692 --> 00:26:35.120 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:26:35.120 --> 00:26:36.920 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, what's
happening in Polish

00:26:36.920 --> 00:26:38.660 align:middle line:90%
is not just "g" becomes "k."

00:26:38.660 --> 00:26:43.290 align:middle line:84%
It's voiced sounds are
becoming voiceless.

00:26:43.290 --> 00:26:45.440 align:middle line:90%
So "z" becomes "s."

00:26:45.440 --> 00:26:47.600 align:middle line:84%
"d" becomes "t."
"b" becomes "p."

00:26:47.600 --> 00:26:48.920 align:middle line:90%
And "g" becomes "k," yeah?

00:26:48.920 --> 00:26:51.590 align:middle line:84%
So it was sometimes
called final devoicing.

00:26:51.590 --> 00:26:54.530 align:middle line:84%
And it's a cross-linguistically
quite common phenomenon.

00:26:54.530 --> 00:26:57.310 align:middle line:90%


00:26:57.310 --> 00:26:58.870 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:26:58.870 --> 00:27:02.920 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:27:02.920 --> 00:27:04.140 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:27:04.140 --> 00:27:07.020 align:middle line:84%
So we talked about
place of articulation.

00:27:07.020 --> 00:27:09.160 align:middle line:90%
And we've talked about voicing.

00:27:09.160 --> 00:27:11.850 align:middle line:84%
Now we need to talk
about another dimension

00:27:11.850 --> 00:27:14.280 align:middle line:84%
for categorizing
sounds, which is

00:27:14.280 --> 00:27:16.060 align:middle line:90%
called manner of articulation.

00:27:16.060 --> 00:27:18.210 align:middle line:90%
So think about "s" and "t."

00:27:18.210 --> 00:27:19.710 align:middle line:90%
They're both alveolar.

00:27:19.710 --> 00:27:22.140 align:middle line:90%
And they're both voiceless.

00:27:22.140 --> 00:27:24.390 align:middle line:84%
But they're different
from each other.

00:27:24.390 --> 00:27:26.760 align:middle line:84%
And the way we distinguish
them is via what

00:27:26.760 --> 00:27:28.600 align:middle line:90%
we call manner of articulation.

00:27:28.600 --> 00:27:31.470 align:middle line:84%
So they're both voiceless
alveolar sounds,

00:27:31.470 --> 00:27:34.200 align:middle line:90%
but "t" is a stop.

00:27:34.200 --> 00:27:37.330 align:middle line:84%
And "s" is what's
called a fricative.

00:27:37.330 --> 00:27:41.280 align:middle line:84%
So stops are also
called plosives.

00:27:41.280 --> 00:27:44.490 align:middle line:84%
I promise never ever
to call them plosives.

00:27:44.490 --> 00:27:46.890 align:middle line:84%
I will always call them
stops because that's

00:27:46.890 --> 00:27:48.060 align:middle line:90%
what I grew up calling them.

00:27:48.060 --> 00:27:50.143 align:middle line:84%
But you will sometimes see
things written in which

00:27:50.143 --> 00:27:51.870 align:middle line:90%
they're called plosives.

00:27:51.870 --> 00:27:54.030 align:middle line:84%
In homeworks, if you ever
need to write about them,

00:27:54.030 --> 00:27:55.520 align:middle line:84%
feel free to call
them either one.

00:27:55.520 --> 00:27:57.240 align:middle line:90%
Doesn't matter.

00:27:57.240 --> 00:27:59.310 align:middle line:84%
I like calling them
stops because they're

00:27:59.310 --> 00:28:02.807 align:middle line:84%
named after the fact that,
well, they stop the flow of air.

00:28:02.807 --> 00:28:03.640 align:middle line:90%
That's what they do.

00:28:03.640 --> 00:28:04.680 align:middle line:90%
That's what a stop is.

00:28:04.680 --> 00:28:06.472 align:middle line:84%
And so the air is coming
out of your lungs.

00:28:06.472 --> 00:28:09.240 align:middle line:90%
And it gets stopped.

00:28:09.240 --> 00:28:12.810 align:middle line:84%
Fricatives like "s" are
sounds in which you don't stop

00:28:12.810 --> 00:28:17.940 align:middle line:84%
the flow of air, but you
narrow some aperture enough

00:28:17.940 --> 00:28:20.130 align:middle line:84%
to create turbulent
air flow, which

00:28:20.130 --> 00:28:22.780 align:middle line:90%
you hear as a hissing sound.

00:28:22.780 --> 00:28:26.460 align:middle line:84%
So sounds like "s" and
"sh" and "f" and "th"--

00:28:26.460 --> 00:28:29.510 align:middle line:90%
these are all fricatives, yeah?

00:28:29.510 --> 00:28:30.010 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:28:30.010 --> 00:28:34.360 align:middle line:90%


00:28:34.360 --> 00:28:37.180 align:middle line:84%
So for "t" and "d,"
the airflow is stopped.

00:28:37.180 --> 00:28:41.230 align:middle line:84%
For "s" and "z," the airflow is
restricted, but is not stopped.

00:28:41.230 --> 00:28:45.040 align:middle line:84%
So you hold your tongue
close to the alveolar ridge,

00:28:45.040 --> 00:28:47.335 align:middle line:84%
but you allow air to
keep flowing through.

00:28:47.335 --> 00:28:49.648 align:middle line:90%


00:28:49.648 --> 00:28:51.190 align:middle line:84%
This is the conversation
we just had.

00:28:51.190 --> 00:28:54.340 align:middle line:84%
That's why an "s,",
you can keep going for

00:28:54.340 --> 00:28:57.700 align:middle line:84%
as long as you have breath
in your lungs, whereas a "t,"

00:28:57.700 --> 00:28:59.140 align:middle line:90%
you can't keep executing it.

00:28:59.140 --> 00:29:01.970 align:middle line:90%


00:29:01.970 --> 00:29:03.270 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:29:03.270 --> 00:29:03.770 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:29:03.770 --> 00:29:08.180 align:middle line:84%
So now we have these
three ways of categorizing

00:29:08.180 --> 00:29:10.640 align:middle line:90%
these kinds of speech sounds--

00:29:10.640 --> 00:29:13.400 align:middle line:90%
place, and manner, and voicing.

00:29:13.400 --> 00:29:16.970 align:middle line:84%
Place of articulation, manner
of articulation, and voicing.

00:29:16.970 --> 00:29:19.370 align:middle line:84%
So there are a bunch of
places of articulation

00:29:19.370 --> 00:29:21.500 align:middle line:90%
over there on the left.

00:29:21.500 --> 00:29:24.350 align:middle line:84%
And the sounds that we
have mainly talked about

00:29:24.350 --> 00:29:26.360 align:middle line:84%
have been either
stops or fricatives.

00:29:26.360 --> 00:29:32.010 align:middle line:84%
And then each of these
places on the table--

00:29:32.010 --> 00:29:34.640 align:middle line:90%
there's a pair of sounds.

00:29:34.640 --> 00:29:37.100 align:middle line:84%
And I hope I managed
to do this right.

00:29:37.100 --> 00:29:39.080 align:middle line:90%
Yes, it looks like I did.

00:29:39.080 --> 00:29:42.800 align:middle line:84%
In all of these pairs,
you've got both a voiced

00:29:42.800 --> 00:29:43.880 align:middle line:90%
and a voiceless sound.

00:29:43.880 --> 00:29:45.858 align:middle line:90%
Which one is first?

00:29:45.858 --> 00:29:46.900 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: The voiced sound

00:29:46.900 --> 00:29:48.567 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: The
voiced sound, yeah.

00:29:48.567 --> 00:29:50.530 align:middle line:84%
So in all of those,
there's a pair.

00:29:50.530 --> 00:29:52.910 align:middle line:84%
So you've got "zh"
and "sh," for example,

00:29:52.910 --> 00:29:54.250 align:middle line:90%
the postalveolar fricatives.

00:29:54.250 --> 00:29:56.020 align:middle line:90%
And the "zh" is the voiced one.

00:29:56.020 --> 00:29:58.120 align:middle line:90%
And the sh is the voiceless one.

00:29:58.120 --> 00:30:00.270 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:30:00.270 --> 00:30:00.870 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

00:30:00.870 --> 00:30:03.640 align:middle line:90%


00:30:03.640 --> 00:30:06.470 align:middle line:84%
All right, so now
new class of sounds.

00:30:06.470 --> 00:30:08.420 align:middle line:84%
This is a new manner
of articulation.

00:30:08.420 --> 00:30:11.170 align:middle line:84%
So we've got "d," which
is a voiced alveolar stop,

00:30:11.170 --> 00:30:14.260 align:middle line:84%
and "z," which is a
voiced alveolar fricative.

00:30:14.260 --> 00:30:16.990 align:middle line:84%
And now we need to
think about "n."

00:30:16.990 --> 00:30:20.860 align:middle line:90%
Well, "n" is voiced--

00:30:20.860 --> 00:30:22.480 align:middle line:90%
nnnnn.

00:30:22.480 --> 00:30:25.600 align:middle line:84%
And it's a stop in
the sense that you

00:30:25.600 --> 00:30:28.582 align:middle line:84%
are stopping the air from
flowing through your mouth.

00:30:28.582 --> 00:30:30.790 align:middle line:84%
If you think about what's
happening inside your mouth

00:30:30.790 --> 00:30:34.150 align:middle line:84%
during an "n," your tongue is
jammed against your alveolar

00:30:34.150 --> 00:30:35.610 align:middle line:90%
ridge-- nnnnn--

00:30:35.610 --> 00:30:42.563 align:middle line:84%
just as it would be for a
"t," So I love the sound

00:30:42.563 --> 00:30:43.730 align:middle line:90%
of phonetics in the morning.

00:30:43.730 --> 00:30:44.508 align:middle line:90%
It's great.

00:30:44.508 --> 00:30:46.460 align:middle line:90%
[CHUCKLING]

00:30:46.460 --> 00:30:50.247 align:middle line:84%
So when you're doing an "n,"
you've stopped the flow of air

00:30:50.247 --> 00:30:50.830 align:middle line:90%
in your mouth.

00:30:50.830 --> 00:30:52.983 align:middle line:84%
And so "n" is technically
a stop, right?

00:30:52.983 --> 00:30:55.150 align:middle line:84%
It's called a stop because
there's no air coming out

00:30:55.150 --> 00:30:55.733 align:middle line:90%
of your mouth.

00:30:55.733 --> 00:30:57.610 align:middle line:84%
But there's air coming
out somewhere, right?

00:30:57.610 --> 00:30:59.724 align:middle line:84%
It's coming out of
your nose, yeah?

00:30:59.724 --> 00:31:00.580 align:middle line:90%
"nnnn."

00:31:00.580 --> 00:31:04.330 align:middle line:84%
And you can tell because if you
hold your nose, [demonstrates]

00:31:04.330 --> 00:31:06.610 align:middle line:84%
you will not be able
to make an "n," yeah?

00:31:06.610 --> 00:31:10.990 align:middle line:84%
So the reason that you can
keep an "n" going, again,

00:31:10.990 --> 00:31:14.320 align:middle line:84%
for as long as you have
breath, is, well, the air

00:31:14.320 --> 00:31:15.340 align:middle line:90%
has someplace to go.

00:31:15.340 --> 00:31:18.010 align:middle line:90%
It's going out your nose.

00:31:18.010 --> 00:31:21.850 align:middle line:84%
What's happening when you make
an "n" is that you are lowering

00:31:21.850 --> 00:31:25.600 align:middle line:84%
the velum, which
is this doohickey

00:31:25.600 --> 00:31:27.970 align:middle line:84%
in the back of your
mouth that partitions

00:31:27.970 --> 00:31:30.820 align:middle line:90%
your nose from your mouth.

00:31:30.820 --> 00:31:34.190 align:middle line:84%
By lowering it, you're allowing
air to flow through your nose.

00:31:34.190 --> 00:31:36.730 align:middle line:84%
So for "t" and
"d," the airflow is

00:31:36.730 --> 00:31:38.140 align:middle line:90%
stopped at the alveolar ridge.

00:31:38.140 --> 00:31:41.800 align:middle line:84%
For "n," the airflow is also
stopped at the alveolar ridge.

00:31:41.800 --> 00:31:43.098 align:middle line:90%
It can't go through the mouth.

00:31:43.098 --> 00:31:44.140 align:middle line:90%
It's stopped right there.

00:31:44.140 --> 00:31:46.240 align:middle line:90%
But it goes through your nose.

00:31:46.240 --> 00:31:51.280 align:middle line:84%
This is a nasal stop, a
voiced alveolar nasal--

00:31:51.280 --> 00:31:55.330 align:middle line:84%
people often just call them
nasals because nasal fricatives

00:31:55.330 --> 00:31:58.020 align:middle line:90%
are messy.

00:31:58.020 --> 00:31:59.160 align:middle line:90%
Actually, not possible.

00:31:59.160 --> 00:32:00.630 align:middle line:90%
Possibly.

00:32:00.630 --> 00:32:01.130 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:32:01.130 --> 00:32:03.810 align:middle line:90%


00:32:03.810 --> 00:32:05.940 align:middle line:90%
So let me go back.

00:32:05.940 --> 00:32:09.210 align:middle line:90%
"n" is an alveolar nasal.

00:32:09.210 --> 00:32:12.585 align:middle line:84%
What would a bilabial
nasal sound like?

00:32:12.585 --> 00:32:14.365 align:middle line:90%
"mmmm," yeah?

00:32:14.365 --> 00:32:15.260 align:middle line:90%
That's an "m."

00:32:15.260 --> 00:32:17.187 align:middle line:84%
You close your lips
and allow the air

00:32:17.187 --> 00:32:18.270 align:middle line:90%
to flow through your nose.

00:32:18.270 --> 00:32:19.545 align:middle line:90%
And a velar nasal?

00:32:19.545 --> 00:32:22.285 align:middle line:90%


00:32:22.285 --> 00:32:24.230 align:middle line:90%
"nnnng."

00:32:24.230 --> 00:32:27.140 align:middle line:84%
Remember that velar means
the place of articulation

00:32:27.140 --> 00:32:28.550 align:middle line:90%
for a "k" or a "g."

00:32:28.550 --> 00:32:30.770 align:middle line:84%
So you make a "k" or
a "g" sound-- kuh--

00:32:30.770 --> 00:32:33.020 align:middle line:84%
and then just let air flow
through your nose-- "nnnn."

00:32:33.020 --> 00:32:38.000 align:middle line:84%
That's the sound at the end of
a word like "song" or "king,"

00:32:38.000 --> 00:32:39.450 align:middle line:90%
making a velar nasal.

00:32:39.450 --> 00:32:39.950 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:32:39.950 --> 00:32:43.400 align:middle line:84%
English doesn't allow words
to begin with velar nasals.

00:32:43.400 --> 00:32:45.440 align:middle line:90%
But there are languages that do.

00:32:45.440 --> 00:32:47.400 align:middle line:90%
So Tagalog, for example--

00:32:47.400 --> 00:32:51.800 align:middle line:90%
the word for now is [TAGALOG].

00:32:51.800 --> 00:32:53.702 align:middle line:90%
So it starts with a velar nasal.

00:32:53.702 --> 00:32:55.910 align:middle line:84%
One of the entertaining
things about learning Tagalog

00:32:55.910 --> 00:32:58.410 align:middle line:84%
is learning how to make sounds
that start with velar nasals.

00:32:58.410 --> 00:33:00.740 align:middle line:84%
If you're an English speaker,
you're not used to it.

00:33:00.740 --> 00:33:01.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:33:01.770 --> 00:33:02.270 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:33:02.270 --> 00:33:05.890 align:middle line:90%


00:33:05.890 --> 00:33:08.410 align:middle line:84%
So there there's
the table again.

00:33:08.410 --> 00:33:10.590 align:middle line:84%
So we've got stops and
fricatives and nasals,

00:33:10.590 --> 00:33:12.850 align:middle line:90%
nasal stops.

00:33:12.850 --> 00:33:14.470 align:middle line:84%
Things can be
voiced or voiceless.

00:33:14.470 --> 00:33:16.990 align:middle line:84%
And we've got all those
places of articulation

00:33:16.990 --> 00:33:18.220 align:middle line:90%
over there on the left.

00:33:18.220 --> 00:33:19.450 align:middle line:84%
Are there questions
about any of this?

00:33:19.450 --> 00:33:21.033 align:middle line:84%
Is anybody looking
at this and saying,

00:33:21.033 --> 00:33:24.570 align:middle line:84%
whoa, this table has grown
out of my ability to keep up?

00:33:24.570 --> 00:33:25.070 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:33:25.070 --> 00:33:25.780 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Question
about the [INAUDIBLE]..

00:33:25.780 --> 00:33:26.170 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:33:26.170 --> 00:33:27.520 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: -- English
doesn't allow?

00:33:27.520 --> 00:33:28.660 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: English
doesn't allow words

00:33:28.660 --> 00:33:30.460 align:middle line:90%
to begin with velar nasals.

00:33:30.460 --> 00:33:31.990 align:middle line:84%
We don't have any
words that start

00:33:31.990 --> 00:33:35.050 align:middle line:84%
with a sound that's at the
end of words like "song."

00:33:35.050 --> 00:33:38.740 align:middle line:84%
We don't have words
like [NON-ENGLISH]..

00:33:38.740 --> 00:33:40.840 align:middle line:84%
English doesn't have
words like that.

00:33:40.840 --> 00:33:43.090 align:middle line:84%
There are plenty of
languages that do.

00:33:43.090 --> 00:33:44.020 align:middle line:90%
Tagalog is one.

00:33:44.020 --> 00:33:45.430 align:middle line:90%
Cantonese is one.

00:33:45.430 --> 00:33:46.580 align:middle line:90%
There's a bunch of others.

00:33:46.580 --> 00:33:49.290 align:middle line:90%
But English doesn't have that.

00:33:49.290 --> 00:33:50.760 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:33:50.760 --> 00:33:53.160 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Well, I
noticed there's not

00:33:53.160 --> 00:33:58.210 align:middle line:84%
"j" in brackets for
[INAUDIBLE] palatal?

00:33:58.210 --> 00:34:01.060 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, yeah.

00:34:01.060 --> 00:34:02.680 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Actually,
I don't actually

00:34:02.680 --> 00:34:05.003 align:middle line:90%
know where this "j" would go.

00:34:05.003 --> 00:34:05.920 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:34:05.920 --> 00:34:09.760 align:middle line:84%
So we haven't yet gotten to the
kind of sound that that "j"-

00:34:09.760 --> 00:34:11.710 align:middle line:84%
the sound of the
beginning of "year."

00:34:11.710 --> 00:34:12.710 align:middle line:90%
You're absolutely right.

00:34:12.710 --> 00:34:14.330 align:middle line:90%
There's a gap there.

00:34:14.330 --> 00:34:17.409 align:middle line:84%
And I've put the palatal
line there partly

00:34:17.409 --> 00:34:19.790 align:middle line:84%
to get you to ask exactly
that kind of question.

00:34:19.790 --> 00:34:22.570 align:middle line:84%
So one of the points
of this table--

00:34:22.570 --> 00:34:25.340 align:middle line:84%
it's like when the periodic
table was first invented,

00:34:25.340 --> 00:34:25.840 align:middle line:90%
I guess.

00:34:25.840 --> 00:34:28.328 align:middle line:84%
So we have the system
for categorizing sounds.

00:34:28.328 --> 00:34:30.370 align:middle line:84%
And now we get to look at
it and say, well, wait.

00:34:30.370 --> 00:34:32.500 align:middle line:84%
Why isn't there
anything there or there?

00:34:32.500 --> 00:34:34.880 align:middle line:84%
And what would it be
like if it were there?

00:34:34.880 --> 00:34:37.872 align:middle line:84%
We'll do a little bit
of that in a second.

00:34:37.872 --> 00:34:38.830 align:middle line:90%
But yeah, you're right.

00:34:38.830 --> 00:34:42.100 align:middle line:84%
So we haven't yet talked
about the kind of sound

00:34:42.100 --> 00:34:45.730 align:middle line:84%
that the IPA symbol "j"--
the sound we usually

00:34:45.730 --> 00:34:47.418 align:middle line:84%
write with "y" in
English, the sound

00:34:47.418 --> 00:34:49.960 align:middle line:84%
of the beginning of "year"-- we
haven't put that on the table

00:34:49.960 --> 00:34:50.460 align:middle line:90%
yet.

00:34:50.460 --> 00:34:51.040 align:middle line:90%
You're right.

00:34:51.040 --> 00:34:52.750 align:middle line:90%
It's not a stop, right?

00:34:52.750 --> 00:34:54.969 align:middle line:84%
It's also not a fricative
because you're not making

00:34:54.969 --> 00:34:56.710 align:middle line:90%
any turbulence in the airflow.

00:34:56.710 --> 00:34:57.970 align:middle line:90%
And it's not nasal.

00:34:57.970 --> 00:34:59.230 align:middle line:90%
So it's another kind of thing.

00:34:59.230 --> 00:35:01.387 align:middle line:90%
We'll get to it.

00:35:01.387 --> 00:35:01.970 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:35:01.970 --> 00:35:05.610 align:middle line:90%
Other questions about this?

00:35:05.610 --> 00:35:09.230 align:middle line:90%
OK, all right.

00:35:09.230 --> 00:35:10.400 align:middle line:90%
So yeah, OK.

00:35:10.400 --> 00:35:12.680 align:middle line:84%
So this way of
classifying sounds

00:35:12.680 --> 00:35:14.150 align:middle line:90%
leads us to wonder about gaps.

00:35:14.150 --> 00:35:14.660 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:35:14.660 --> 00:35:15.320 align:middle line:90%
Thanks, Norman.

00:35:15.320 --> 00:35:16.528 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, you set that up nicely.

00:35:16.528 --> 00:35:19.640 align:middle line:90%


00:35:19.640 --> 00:35:22.040 align:middle line:84%
So let's think about
some of these gaps.

00:35:22.040 --> 00:35:26.210 align:middle line:84%
So English, for example, has
bilabial stops, "p" and "b."

00:35:26.210 --> 00:35:28.940 align:middle line:84%
And it has a bilabial
nasal, an "m."

00:35:28.940 --> 00:35:32.430 align:middle line:84%
But it doesn't have
a bilabial fricative.

00:35:32.430 --> 00:35:35.655 align:middle line:84%
What would a labial
fricative sound like?

00:35:35.655 --> 00:35:39.985 align:middle line:84%
Fh, fh, which sounds like
blowing out a candle, right?

00:35:39.985 --> 00:35:42.110 align:middle line:84%
Open your lips just enough
to let some air come out

00:35:42.110 --> 00:35:43.092 align:middle line:90%
and then blow.

00:35:43.092 --> 00:35:43.800 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [TRILLS]

00:35:43.800 --> 00:35:45.410 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Ah,
that's a bilabial trill.

00:35:45.410 --> 00:35:46.310 align:middle line:90%
We will get to that.

00:35:46.310 --> 00:35:50.190 align:middle line:84%
[LAUGHS] Either that or
you were just having fun.

00:35:50.190 --> 00:35:51.890 align:middle line:90%
I'm not sure.

00:35:51.890 --> 00:35:54.700 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
that have that.

00:35:54.700 --> 00:35:55.200 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:35:55.200 --> 00:35:57.020 align:middle line:84%
So bilabial fricative--
English doesn't

00:35:57.020 --> 00:35:58.220 align:middle line:90%
have a bilabial fricative.

00:35:58.220 --> 00:35:59.870 align:middle line:90%
But there are languages that do.

00:35:59.870 --> 00:36:01.350 align:middle line:90%
Japanese does, for example.

00:36:01.350 --> 00:36:05.000 align:middle line:84%
So in Japanese, when people were
writing Japanese in the Roman

00:36:05.000 --> 00:36:07.880 align:middle line:84%
alphabet, when they
write an "f," like when

00:36:07.880 --> 00:36:10.272 align:middle line:84%
they write the name
of this mountain,

00:36:10.272 --> 00:36:11.480 align:middle line:90%
they'll write it with an "f."

00:36:11.480 --> 00:36:13.280 align:middle line:84%
But in Japanese,
that's not an "f."

00:36:13.280 --> 00:36:14.450 align:middle line:90%
It's a bilabial fricative.

00:36:14.450 --> 00:36:16.250 align:middle line:90%
It's "Fhuji," "Fhuji."

00:36:16.250 --> 00:36:18.200 align:middle line:84%
If you learn Japanese,
you must learn

00:36:18.200 --> 00:36:23.210 align:middle line:84%
to pronounce the "f" bilabially
rather than labiodentally.

00:36:23.210 --> 00:36:27.320 align:middle line:84%
So in English, we have a
labiodental "f" with our lower

00:36:27.320 --> 00:36:28.820 align:middle line:90%
lip against our teeth.

00:36:28.820 --> 00:36:31.400 align:middle line:84%
In Japanese "f," your
teeth are not involved.

00:36:31.400 --> 00:36:32.972 align:middle line:90%
It is only your lips, yeah?

00:36:32.972 --> 00:36:35.710 align:middle line:90%


00:36:35.710 --> 00:36:36.580 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:36:36.580 --> 00:36:39.610 align:middle line:84%
What would a buh bilabial
fricative sound like?

00:36:39.610 --> 00:36:41.920 align:middle line:84%
Is this a voiced or a
voiceless bilabial fricative?

00:36:41.920 --> 00:36:42.712 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Voiceless.

00:36:42.712 --> 00:36:43.837 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Voiceless.

00:36:43.837 --> 00:36:44.350 align:middle line:90%
Fh, fh.

00:36:44.350 --> 00:36:46.850 align:middle line:84%
What would it sound
like if it were voiced?

00:36:46.850 --> 00:36:48.610 align:middle line:90%
Vh, vh.

00:36:48.610 --> 00:36:50.128 align:middle line:90%
And that exists, too.

00:36:50.128 --> 00:36:51.920 align:middle line:84%
There are dialects of
Spanish, for example,

00:36:51.920 --> 00:36:54.490 align:middle line:84%
that have that
between vowels, if you

00:36:54.490 --> 00:36:59.650 align:middle line:84%
have the letter B between
vowels in words like "abuela."

00:36:59.650 --> 00:37:02.840 align:middle line:90%
That "b" has that sound--

00:37:02.840 --> 00:37:06.360 align:middle line:90%
a voiced bilabial fricative.

00:37:06.360 --> 00:37:09.480 align:middle line:84%
Moving across the chart,
I've got a nasal stop there.

00:37:09.480 --> 00:37:13.450 align:middle line:84%
Is that nasal
voiced or voiceless?

00:37:13.450 --> 00:37:13.960 align:middle line:90%
Voiced.

00:37:13.960 --> 00:37:15.550 align:middle line:90%
"m."

00:37:15.550 --> 00:37:18.910 align:middle line:84%
What would it sound like
if it were voiceless?

00:37:18.910 --> 00:37:21.920 align:middle line:90%
I should step back.

00:37:21.920 --> 00:37:23.720 align:middle line:90%
[EXHALES SHARPLY]

00:37:23.720 --> 00:37:25.070 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, English doesn't have that.

00:37:25.070 --> 00:37:28.760 align:middle line:84%
[EXHALES SHARPLY] But there are
languages out there that do,

00:37:28.760 --> 00:37:31.830 align:middle line:84%
languages like
Hmong, for example,

00:37:31.830 --> 00:37:33.983 align:middle line:84%
which is a minority
language spoken in--

00:37:33.983 --> 00:37:35.900 align:middle line:84%
actually, it's quite a
large minority language

00:37:35.900 --> 00:37:38.260 align:middle line:90%
spoken in Vietnam.

00:37:38.260 --> 00:37:42.230 align:middle line:90%
Hmong has that kind of sound.

00:37:42.230 --> 00:37:45.850 align:middle line:84%
Tibetan has that
kind of sound, too.

00:37:45.850 --> 00:37:46.930 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:37:46.930 --> 00:37:49.960 align:middle line:90%
Let's skip palatal and do velar.

00:37:49.960 --> 00:37:52.480 align:middle line:84%
We've got velar stops in
English-- "k" and "g,"

00:37:52.480 --> 00:37:53.590 align:middle line:90%
kuh and guh.

00:37:53.590 --> 00:37:56.110 align:middle line:84%
And we've got
velar nasals, nnnn.

00:37:56.110 --> 00:38:00.190 align:middle line:84%
What would a velar
fricative sound like?

00:38:00.190 --> 00:38:01.460 align:middle line:90%
Kh-- yes?

00:38:01.460 --> 00:38:03.638 align:middle line:84%
She's alerting me that she's
not just hissing at me.

00:38:03.638 --> 00:38:04.930 align:middle line:90%
She's making a velar fricative.

00:38:04.930 --> 00:38:05.430 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:38:05.430 --> 00:38:08.590 align:middle line:90%
Kh, kh, right?

00:38:08.590 --> 00:38:10.648 align:middle line:84%
Is that a voiced or a
voiceless velar fricative?

00:38:10.648 --> 00:38:11.440 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Voiceless.

00:38:11.440 --> 00:38:12.300 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Voiceless, yeah?

00:38:12.300 --> 00:38:13.200 align:middle line:90%
Kh.

00:38:13.200 --> 00:38:13.870 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:38:13.870 --> 00:38:15.430 align:middle line:90%
English doesn't have that.

00:38:15.430 --> 00:38:17.350 align:middle line:84%
But there are
languages that do--

00:38:17.350 --> 00:38:18.550 align:middle line:90%
languages like German, say.

00:38:18.550 --> 00:38:21.008 align:middle line:84%
That's one of the sounds that
they write with the letters C

00:38:21.008 --> 00:38:25.300 align:middle line:84%
and H at the end of composers'
names, like Bach, right?

00:38:25.300 --> 00:38:27.340 align:middle line:90%
Johann Sebastian Bach.

00:38:27.340 --> 00:38:29.710 align:middle line:84%
His name ends with
a velar fricative.

00:38:29.710 --> 00:38:32.500 align:middle line:90%
Russian has this sound, yeah?

00:38:32.500 --> 00:38:36.580 align:middle line:84%
It shows up in the names
of authors like Chekhov,

00:38:36.580 --> 00:38:39.380 align:middle line:90%
that kh sound.

00:38:39.380 --> 00:38:42.145 align:middle line:84%
What would a velar fricative
sound like if it were voiced?

00:38:42.145 --> 00:38:44.990 align:middle line:90%


00:38:44.990 --> 00:38:46.990 align:middle line:90%
Lhg.

00:38:46.990 --> 00:38:47.990 align:middle line:90%
Lhg.

00:38:47.990 --> 00:38:50.240 align:middle line:84%
And English doesn't
have that, either.

00:38:50.240 --> 00:38:51.860 align:middle line:90%
But there are languages that do.

00:38:51.860 --> 00:38:53.360 align:middle line:84%
Again, there are
dialects of Spanish

00:38:53.360 --> 00:38:55.160 align:middle line:84%
where if you have a
"g" between vowels,

00:38:55.160 --> 00:39:00.110 align:middle line:84%
it'll get this kind of sound,
in words like "agua," yeah?

00:39:00.110 --> 00:39:01.780 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:39:01.780 --> 00:39:04.630 align:middle line:84%
So we've talked about
various kinds of nasal stops.

00:39:04.630 --> 00:39:10.570 align:middle line:84%
So an "m' is a bilabial nasal,
where you close the flow of air

00:39:10.570 --> 00:39:13.300 align:middle line:84%
at your lips and
allow the air to flow

00:39:13.300 --> 00:39:15.620 align:middle line:84%
through your nasal cavity
by lowering your velum.

00:39:15.620 --> 00:39:17.290 align:middle line:90%
So you get mmmmm.

00:39:17.290 --> 00:39:19.360 align:middle line:84%
Or you can stop the flow
of air in other places.

00:39:19.360 --> 00:39:21.580 align:middle line:84%
You can have an alveolar
nasal, right? "n."

00:39:21.580 --> 00:39:23.770 align:middle line:84%
You can have a
velar nasal-- "ng."

00:39:23.770 --> 00:39:25.780 align:middle line:84%
What would a glottal
nasal sound like?

00:39:25.780 --> 00:39:30.640 align:middle line:90%


00:39:30.640 --> 00:39:31.330 align:middle line:90%
Trick question.

00:39:31.330 --> 00:39:32.560 align:middle line:90%
You would need surgery.

00:39:32.560 --> 00:39:33.910 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:39:33.910 --> 00:39:39.580 align:middle line:84%
So the way nasal stops work is
you're stopping the flow of air

00:39:39.580 --> 00:39:41.320 align:middle line:90%
somewhere in your vocal tract.

00:39:41.320 --> 00:39:44.480 align:middle line:84%
But you're allowing the air to
flow through your nasal cavity,

00:39:44.480 --> 00:39:44.980 align:middle line:90%
right?

00:39:44.980 --> 00:39:46.840 align:middle line:90%
That's what a nasal stop is.

00:39:46.840 --> 00:39:50.170 align:middle line:84%
Here, let's get back to one
of these sagittal sections.

00:39:50.170 --> 00:39:53.500 align:middle line:90%
So that "n" there--

00:39:53.500 --> 00:39:55.840 align:middle line:84%
what you're doing is you're
stopping the flow of air

00:39:55.840 --> 00:39:57.473 align:middle line:84%
there at the alveolar
ridge, right?

00:39:57.473 --> 00:39:59.890 align:middle line:84%
But you're lowering the velum
to let the air flow through.

00:39:59.890 --> 00:40:03.280 align:middle line:84%
Or an "m"-- you'd be closing
the flow of air at your lips

00:40:03.280 --> 00:40:05.500 align:middle line:84%
and lowering your velum to
let the air flow through.

00:40:05.500 --> 00:40:09.580 align:middle line:84%
Or velar nasal, "ng,"
like at the end of "king,"

00:40:09.580 --> 00:40:11.362 align:middle line:84%
you're making a
closure at the velum.

00:40:11.362 --> 00:40:12.820 align:middle line:84%
But you're also
lowering the volume

00:40:12.820 --> 00:40:14.500 align:middle line:84%
and letting the
air flow through.

00:40:14.500 --> 00:40:18.190 align:middle line:84%
A glottal nasal-- you'd
have to stop the flow of air

00:40:18.190 --> 00:40:24.220 align:middle line:84%
down there at the vocal
folds and let the air

00:40:24.220 --> 00:40:26.128 align:middle line:90%
go through your velum.

00:40:26.128 --> 00:40:27.670 align:middle line:84%
But if you're stopping
it down there,

00:40:27.670 --> 00:40:29.900 align:middle line:84%
it can't be going
through your velum.

00:40:29.900 --> 00:40:32.710 align:middle line:84%
So you would need, again,
as I say, probably there

00:40:32.710 --> 00:40:35.257 align:middle line:84%
are unethical surgeons who
would modify you so that you

00:40:35.257 --> 00:40:36.340 align:middle line:90%
could make glottal nasals.

00:40:36.340 --> 00:40:38.500 align:middle line:84%
You'd need extra ways
to get air to flow

00:40:38.500 --> 00:40:40.120 align:middle line:90%
through your nasal cavity.

00:40:40.120 --> 00:40:43.340 align:middle line:84%
I'm not recommending
this, by the way.

00:40:43.340 --> 00:40:45.040 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:40:45.040 --> 00:40:47.840 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:40:47.840 --> 00:40:48.340 align:middle line:90%
Cool.

00:40:48.340 --> 00:40:50.940 align:middle line:90%


00:40:50.940 --> 00:40:53.850 align:middle line:90%
So those are some of the gaps.

00:40:53.850 --> 00:40:56.790 align:middle line:84%
Oh, and I flagged a gap
that I didn't talk about.

00:40:56.790 --> 00:40:58.830 align:middle line:90%
Sorry, a couple of gaps.

00:40:58.830 --> 00:41:03.390 align:middle line:84%
So English has interdental
fricatives-- thuh and thuh.

00:41:03.390 --> 00:41:05.880 align:middle line:84%
And as I said, those are
cross linguistically rare,

00:41:05.880 --> 00:41:10.830 align:middle line:84%
which is why if you're trying to
do an accent from various kinds

00:41:10.830 --> 00:41:12.880 align:middle line:84%
of places, for
example, in Europe,

00:41:12.880 --> 00:41:15.510 align:middle line:84%
one of the things you do
is replace your th sounds

00:41:15.510 --> 00:41:16.810 align:middle line:90%
with other kinds of sounds.

00:41:16.810 --> 00:41:19.740 align:middle line:84%
So if you're doing a French
accent, you replace your "th"

00:41:19.740 --> 00:41:22.350 align:middle line:90%
sounds with "z"s, right?

00:41:22.350 --> 00:41:24.750 align:middle line:84%
Or if you're doing a German
accent, you replace your "th"

00:41:24.750 --> 00:41:26.970 align:middle line:90%
sounds with "t"s, yeah?

00:41:26.970 --> 00:41:29.430 align:middle line:84%
English has
interdental fricatives.

00:41:29.430 --> 00:41:33.205 align:middle line:84%
And it has alveolar
stops, "t" and "d."

00:41:33.205 --> 00:41:34.830 align:middle line:84%
There are languages
out there that have

00:41:34.830 --> 00:41:38.500 align:middle line:90%
what are called dental stops.

00:41:38.500 --> 00:41:40.680 align:middle line:84%
So the alveolar stop,
again, involves your tongue

00:41:40.680 --> 00:41:43.440 align:middle line:84%
touching the alveolar ridge and
stopping the flow of air there.

00:41:43.440 --> 00:41:46.450 align:middle line:84%
For a dental stop, your
tongue is touching your teeth.

00:41:46.450 --> 00:41:48.360 align:middle line:84%
So you're not saying
[NON-ENGLISH],,

00:41:48.360 --> 00:41:52.222 align:middle line:90%
you're saying [NON-ENGLISH].

00:41:52.222 --> 00:41:53.890 align:middle line:90%
English does not have those.

00:41:53.890 --> 00:41:56.070 align:middle line:84%
But there are languages
out there that do.

00:41:56.070 --> 00:41:58.153 align:middle line:84%
If you're studying
another language,

00:41:58.153 --> 00:41:59.820 align:middle line:84%
this is the kind of
thing to think about

00:41:59.820 --> 00:42:01.487 align:middle line:84%
because sometimes,
your teacher will not

00:42:01.487 --> 00:42:02.820 align:middle line:90%
be thinking about this.

00:42:02.820 --> 00:42:05.520 align:middle line:84%
But you should be asking
yourself, is this sound,

00:42:05.520 --> 00:42:07.470 align:middle line:84%
the sound that
sounds like a "t"--

00:42:07.470 --> 00:42:10.410 align:middle line:84%
is it an alveolar "t'
or is it a dental "t'?

00:42:10.410 --> 00:42:13.030 align:middle line:84%
Part of your job, if you're
learning Tagalog, for example,

00:42:13.030 --> 00:42:15.750 align:middle line:84%
is to learn to make dental
"t"s instead of alveolar "t"

00:42:15.750 --> 00:42:17.920 align:middle line:90%
because that's what they've got.

00:42:17.920 --> 00:42:20.280 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:42:20.280 --> 00:42:23.520 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: If you're thinking
about linguistic "t,"

00:42:23.520 --> 00:42:25.650 align:middle line:90%
but let's say someone doesn't--

00:42:25.650 --> 00:42:27.090 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Have teeth?

00:42:27.090 --> 00:42:27.630 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

00:42:27.630 --> 00:42:29.932 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:42:29.932 --> 00:42:31.140 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: How would that work?

00:42:31.140 --> 00:42:32.100 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Well--

00:42:32.100 --> 00:42:34.125 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Would it
be a [INAUDIBLE]??

00:42:34.125 --> 00:42:37.590 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: [LAUGHS] I
believe that is what people do,

00:42:37.590 --> 00:42:38.130 align:middle line:90%
yeah.

00:42:38.130 --> 00:42:40.900 align:middle line:90%
So you make a closure.

00:42:40.900 --> 00:42:42.900 align:middle line:84%
We can talk about interdental
fricatives, right?

00:42:42.900 --> 00:42:46.680 align:middle line:84%
So somebody who doesn't have
teeth and wants to do a thuh--

00:42:46.680 --> 00:42:47.400 align:middle line:90%
what do they do?

00:42:47.400 --> 00:42:51.480 align:middle line:84%
I think their job is to
create a turbulent airflow

00:42:51.480 --> 00:42:54.300 align:middle line:84%
with their tongue between
where their teeth would be

00:42:54.300 --> 00:42:56.868 align:middle line:90%
if they only had teeth, right?

00:42:56.868 --> 00:42:58.410 align:middle line:84%
And I think there's
something similar

00:42:58.410 --> 00:42:59.688 align:middle line:90%
going on with dental stops.

00:42:59.688 --> 00:43:01.230 align:middle line:84%
But this is exactly
the kind of thing

00:43:01.230 --> 00:43:03.090 align:middle line:84%
that experimental
phoneticists do

00:43:03.090 --> 00:43:05.160 align:middle line:84%
is to try to figure
out one of the things--

00:43:05.160 --> 00:43:06.570 align:middle line:84%
they do many
things, some of them

00:43:06.570 --> 00:43:08.040 align:middle line:84%
creepy, to try to
figure out what's

00:43:08.040 --> 00:43:12.360 align:middle line:84%
going on in your vocal tract
exactly as you do this stuff.

00:43:12.360 --> 00:43:15.060 align:middle line:84%
There's all this
work on what people

00:43:15.060 --> 00:43:17.910 align:middle line:84%
do to compensate for various
kinds of obstructions

00:43:17.910 --> 00:43:18.730 align:middle line:90%
in the vocal tract.

00:43:18.730 --> 00:43:20.490 align:middle line:84%
So you have people
bite on a block.

00:43:20.490 --> 00:43:22.898 align:middle line:84%
And you put something
solid in their mouth.

00:43:22.898 --> 00:43:23.940 align:middle line:90%
And then you're like, OK.

00:43:23.940 --> 00:43:26.520 align:middle line:84%
So now what will you
do with your tongue

00:43:26.520 --> 00:43:29.520 align:middle line:84%
to make the sounds
as best you can?

00:43:29.520 --> 00:43:31.770 align:middle line:84%
All kinds of weird
stuff people do.

00:43:31.770 --> 00:43:33.000 align:middle line:90%
Cool stuff.

00:43:33.000 --> 00:43:34.750 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:43:34.750 --> 00:43:37.357 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:43:37.357 --> 00:43:38.440 align:middle line:90%
Where was I with all that?

00:43:38.440 --> 00:43:39.010 align:middle line:90%
OK, yeah.

00:43:39.010 --> 00:43:43.490 align:middle line:84%
So yeah, that's another
set of IPA symbols.

00:43:43.490 --> 00:43:45.100 align:middle line:84%
These are your first
IPA diacritics,

00:43:45.100 --> 00:43:47.530 align:middle line:84%
I guess, those little square
doohickeys under the "t"

00:43:47.530 --> 00:43:48.580 align:middle line:90%
and the "d" there.

00:43:48.580 --> 00:43:51.055 align:middle line:84%
Those indicate that that
particular "t" and "d" those

00:43:51.055 --> 00:43:52.780 align:middle line:90%
are dental.

00:43:52.780 --> 00:43:55.810 align:middle line:84%
There are even languages out
there that have both dental

00:43:55.810 --> 00:43:57.730 align:middle line:90%
"t"s and alveolar "t's.

00:43:57.730 --> 00:44:00.730 align:middle line:84%
So the Dravidian
languages of India

00:44:00.730 --> 00:44:02.200 align:middle line:90%
are famous for having those.

00:44:02.200 --> 00:44:06.010 align:middle line:84%
And a lot of the Aboriginal
languages of Australia

00:44:06.010 --> 00:44:09.393 align:middle line:84%
are really rich in
places of articulation.

00:44:09.393 --> 00:44:10.810 align:middle line:84%
So is Dinka, come
to think of it--

00:44:10.810 --> 00:44:13.180 align:middle line:84%
this Nilotic language
that I mentioned briefly

00:44:13.180 --> 00:44:17.132 align:middle line:84%
that has tone as a
way of marking case.

00:44:17.132 --> 00:44:19.090 align:middle line:84%
These are all languages
that have lots and lots

00:44:19.090 --> 00:44:20.650 align:middle line:84%
of places of
articulation, including

00:44:20.650 --> 00:44:23.890 align:middle line:84%
both dental and
alveolar, but that often

00:44:23.890 --> 00:44:26.170 align:middle line:84%
don't make the languages
of Australia-- at least,

00:44:26.170 --> 00:44:28.633 align:middle line:84%
often don't make
voicing distinctions.

00:44:28.633 --> 00:44:30.550 align:middle line:84%
So they have stops, but
they don't distinguish

00:44:30.550 --> 00:44:32.210 align:middle line:90%
voiced from voiceless.

00:44:32.210 --> 00:44:36.550 align:middle line:84%
So they have places of
articulation and lots of them,

00:44:36.550 --> 00:44:39.220 align:middle line:84%
and nasals in all those places
as well, but no distinction

00:44:39.220 --> 00:44:41.460 align:middle line:90%
between voiced and voiceless.

00:44:41.460 --> 00:44:42.860 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:44:42.860 --> 00:44:44.630 align:middle line:84%
OK, any other
questions about this

00:44:44.630 --> 00:44:48.740 align:middle line:90%
chart before we zoom past it?

00:44:48.740 --> 00:44:50.840 align:middle line:84%
Like I say, on
the website, there

00:44:50.840 --> 00:44:55.910 align:middle line:84%
will be a link to charts
that will look, hopefully,

00:44:55.910 --> 00:44:59.630 align:middle line:84%
like this, more official
charts by the IPA,

00:44:59.630 --> 00:45:03.860 align:middle line:84%
which will have sound files so
that you can listen to trained

00:45:03.860 --> 00:45:05.450 align:middle line:90%
phonologists making the sounds.

00:45:05.450 --> 00:45:08.840 align:middle line:84%
And also, at least one website,
which I hope is still up,

00:45:08.840 --> 00:45:12.230 align:middle line:84%
has MRIs so that you can
watch the inside of a person's

00:45:12.230 --> 00:45:16.210 align:middle line:84%
vocal tract as they
make the sound.

00:45:16.210 --> 00:45:18.570 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:45:18.570 --> 00:45:22.680 align:middle line:84%
Now we have been talking
about parts of the vocal tract

00:45:22.680 --> 00:45:24.435 align:middle line:90%
that English uses.

00:45:24.435 --> 00:45:26.560 align:middle line:84%
Sometimes, it doesn't use
them for the same things.

00:45:26.560 --> 00:45:27.360 align:middle line:90%
Other languages do.

00:45:27.360 --> 00:45:28.180 align:middle line:90%
So we have teeth.

00:45:28.180 --> 00:45:29.640 align:middle line:84%
We use our teeth
for interdentals,

00:45:29.640 --> 00:45:31.800 align:middle line:90%
but we don't make dental stops.

00:45:31.800 --> 00:45:34.920 align:middle line:84%
But there are places in the
vocal tract that English just

00:45:34.920 --> 00:45:35.670 align:middle line:90%
does not use.

00:45:35.670 --> 00:45:37.890 align:middle line:90%
And yet other languages do.

00:45:37.890 --> 00:45:39.180 align:middle line:90%
Here are a couple.

00:45:39.180 --> 00:45:41.380 align:middle line:84%
There are what are
called retroflex sounds.

00:45:41.380 --> 00:45:43.380 align:middle line:84%
These are sounds in which
the tip of your tongue

00:45:43.380 --> 00:45:44.650 align:middle line:90%
is on your palate.

00:45:44.650 --> 00:45:48.180 align:middle line:84%
So instead of a tuh,
you're making a cuh--

00:45:48.180 --> 00:45:49.960 align:middle line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH].

00:45:49.960 --> 00:45:51.990 align:middle line:84%
So your tongue is
curled back a little bit

00:45:51.990 --> 00:45:53.820 align:middle line:84%
further than it
would be for a "t"

00:45:53.820 --> 00:45:56.490 align:middle line:84%
And it's making a closure,
if you're making a stop,

00:45:56.490 --> 00:45:57.280 align:middle line:90%
right there.

00:45:57.280 --> 00:45:58.798 align:middle line:90%
So you can make stops there--

00:45:58.798 --> 00:46:00.090 align:middle line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH] or [NON-ENGLISH].

00:46:00.090 --> 00:46:02.370 align:middle line:84%
You can make fricatives
there, like [NON-ENGLISH]

00:46:02.370 --> 00:46:04.380 align:middle line:90%
or [NON-ENGLISH].

00:46:04.380 --> 00:46:09.150 align:middle line:84%
And you can make nasals there,
like are [NON-ENGLISH],, yeah?

00:46:09.150 --> 00:46:11.550 align:middle line:84%
Retroflex sounds are
very popular in India,

00:46:11.550 --> 00:46:16.500 align:middle line:90%
and Australia, and Indonesia.

00:46:16.500 --> 00:46:17.640 align:middle line:90%
They're all over the place.

00:46:17.640 --> 00:46:18.940 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:46:18.940 --> 00:46:22.770 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Um, can there be--
there's a retroflex lateral,

00:46:22.770 --> 00:46:23.776 align:middle line:90%
too, right?

00:46:23.776 --> 00:46:26.310 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
[NON-ENGLISH] Yeah, mm-hmm.

00:46:26.310 --> 00:46:26.898 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:46:26.898 --> 00:46:28.440 align:middle line:84%
Well, we'll get to
laterals, but yes.

00:46:28.440 --> 00:46:29.500 align:middle line:90%
Yes, there is.

00:46:29.500 --> 00:46:30.680 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:46:30.680 --> 00:46:32.964 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:46:32.964 --> 00:46:35.100 align:middle line:90%
OK, those are retroflexes.

00:46:35.100 --> 00:46:40.060 align:middle line:84%
Uvulars are kind of
like "k" except more so.

00:46:40.060 --> 00:46:42.490 align:middle line:84%
So for "k," your
back of your tongue

00:46:42.490 --> 00:46:45.108 align:middle line:84%
is hitting the
back of your mouth.

00:46:45.108 --> 00:46:46.150 align:middle line:90%
It's touching your velum.

00:46:46.150 --> 00:46:47.230 align:middle line:90%
That's a "k" sound--

00:46:47.230 --> 00:46:48.190 align:middle line:90%
[NON-ENGLISH].

00:46:48.190 --> 00:46:50.290 align:middle line:84%
For a uvular, your
tongue goes further back.

00:46:50.290 --> 00:46:52.413 align:middle line:84%
And it gets at or
near your uvula,

00:46:52.413 --> 00:46:53.830 align:middle line:84%
which is the little
doohickey that

00:46:53.830 --> 00:46:57.070 align:middle line:90%
hangs down there at the back.

00:46:57.070 --> 00:46:58.040 align:middle line:90%
That's your uvula.

00:46:58.040 --> 00:47:02.260 align:middle line:84%
So this is a sound
like [NON-ENGLISH],,

00:47:02.260 --> 00:47:06.340 align:middle line:84%
or [NON-ENGLISH],,
or [NON-ENGLISH],,

00:47:06.340 --> 00:47:09.100 align:middle line:84%
or [NON-ENGLISH],,
or [NON-ENGLISH]..

00:47:09.100 --> 00:47:12.673 align:middle line:90%
These are all uvular sounds.

00:47:12.673 --> 00:47:14.590 align:middle line:84%
We do not have these in
English, at least when

00:47:14.590 --> 00:47:15.382 align:middle line:90%
we're feeling well.

00:47:15.382 --> 00:47:18.130 align:middle line:84%
But there are languages
out there the do.

00:47:18.130 --> 00:47:23.170 align:middle line:84%
You're working on
a problem set that

00:47:23.170 --> 00:47:25.360 align:middle line:90%
involves the language Inupiaq.

00:47:25.360 --> 00:47:29.080 align:middle line:84%
And that letter "k" is a
symbol for a uvular stop.

00:47:29.080 --> 00:47:31.060 align:middle line:90%
Inupiaq has a uvular stop.

00:47:31.060 --> 00:47:32.650 align:middle line:84%
It has one at the
end of the name.

00:47:32.650 --> 00:47:34.330 align:middle line:84%
The Inu at the
beginning means people.

00:47:34.330 --> 00:47:38.320 align:middle line:84%
And piaq is a suffix meaning
something like real, or normal,

00:47:38.320 --> 00:47:39.310 align:middle line:90%
or regular, right?

00:47:39.310 --> 00:47:41.560 align:middle line:84%
So the Inupiaq are
the normal people.

00:47:41.560 --> 00:47:43.300 align:middle line:84%
The rest of us are
abnormal, yeah.

00:47:43.300 --> 00:47:46.490 align:middle line:90%


00:47:46.490 --> 00:47:46.990 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:47:46.990 --> 00:47:51.880 align:middle line:90%
Uvulars-- reasonably popular.

00:47:51.880 --> 00:47:52.480 align:middle line:90%
Let's see.

00:47:52.480 --> 00:47:56.380 align:middle line:84%
They're all over the Indigenous
languages of the Americas.

00:47:56.380 --> 00:47:57.625 align:middle line:90%
They have them in Arabic.

00:47:57.625 --> 00:48:00.220 align:middle line:90%


00:48:00.220 --> 00:48:02.860 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, they're not exactly rare.

00:48:02.860 --> 00:48:05.230 align:middle line:90%
But English does not have them.

00:48:05.230 --> 00:48:09.200 align:middle line:90%
The uvular voiced fricative--

00:48:09.200 --> 00:48:15.100 align:middle line:84%
so the voiceless fricative,
the "kh," "kh," is found,

00:48:15.100 --> 00:48:17.710 align:middle line:84%
but not usually
common in Europe.

00:48:17.710 --> 00:48:22.720 align:middle line:84%
The "gh" "gh"-- the
voiced uvular fricative--

00:48:22.720 --> 00:48:26.080 align:middle line:84%
is one of the ways
to pronounce an "r"

00:48:26.080 --> 00:48:28.360 align:middle line:84%
in languages like French
and German, right?

00:48:28.360 --> 00:48:31.330 align:middle line:84%
So if you're pronouncing
the French word for "red,"

00:48:31.330 --> 00:48:33.370 align:middle line:84%
one of the ways to do it
is with this fricative,

00:48:33.370 --> 00:48:34.800 align:middle line:90%
to say "rouge"--

00:48:34.800 --> 00:48:35.300 align:middle line:90%
"rgh".

00:48:35.300 --> 00:48:39.875 align:middle line:84%
That "rgh" sound is a
voiced uvular fricative.

00:48:39.875 --> 00:48:40.750 align:middle line:90%
There are other ways.

00:48:40.750 --> 00:48:42.992 align:middle line:90%
You can also do a uvular trill--

00:48:42.992 --> 00:48:49.400 align:middle line:84%
[TRILLS]---- where you get your
uvula to flap in the breeze,

00:48:49.400 --> 00:48:52.042 align:middle line:90%
but not everybody does that.

00:48:52.042 --> 00:48:52.990 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:48:52.990 --> 00:48:54.160 align:middle line:90%
Questions about that?

00:48:54.160 --> 00:48:55.440 align:middle line:90%
Accents?

00:48:55.440 --> 00:48:57.590 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:48:57.590 --> 00:49:02.170 align:middle line:84%
And then there are
also pharyngeals.

00:49:02.170 --> 00:49:06.820 align:middle line:84%
Pharyngeals involve constriction
near the pharyngeal wall.

00:49:06.820 --> 00:49:08.577 align:middle line:90%
Arabic has these.

00:49:08.577 --> 00:49:09.910 align:middle line:90%
The Berber languages have these.

00:49:09.910 --> 00:49:13.607 align:middle line:84%
These are like, "ah, hah,
ah, hah," or "ah, ah."

00:49:13.607 --> 00:49:15.190 align:middle line:84%
You're getting the
back of your tongue

00:49:15.190 --> 00:49:17.930 align:middle line:84%
to get against the back
of your vocal tract.

00:49:17.930 --> 00:49:20.410 align:middle line:90%
Does anybody here speak Arabic?

00:49:20.410 --> 00:49:22.410 align:middle line:84%
OK, so if you know
anyone who speaks Arabic,

00:49:22.410 --> 00:49:23.490 align:middle line:84%
get them to speak
to you for a while

00:49:23.490 --> 00:49:25.230 align:middle line:84%
and you'll get to
hear them saying this.

00:49:25.230 --> 00:49:26.688 align:middle line:84%
As I say, there
will be sound files

00:49:26.688 --> 00:49:30.400 align:middle line:84%
where you'll get to hear
people making these.

00:49:30.400 --> 00:49:31.650 align:middle line:90%
So English doesn't have these.

00:49:31.650 --> 00:49:35.280 align:middle line:90%
But there are languages that do.

00:49:35.280 --> 00:49:37.080 align:middle line:90%
There are pharyngeal fricatives.

00:49:37.080 --> 00:49:39.250 align:middle line:84%
Those are both
pharyngeal fricatives.

00:49:39.250 --> 00:49:42.600 align:middle line:84%
OK, so slightly larger chart
with more symbols on it,

00:49:42.600 --> 00:49:45.100 align:middle line:84%
including some of the ones we've
talked about-- pharyngeals,

00:49:45.100 --> 00:49:47.920 align:middle line:90%
uvulars, retroflexes.

00:49:47.920 --> 00:49:50.140 align:middle line:84%
And for some reason, the
dental stops are still red.

00:49:50.140 --> 00:49:51.260 align:middle line:90%
I don't know why.

00:49:51.260 --> 00:49:52.560 align:middle line:90%
Have to fix that.

00:49:52.560 --> 00:49:53.580 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

00:49:53.580 --> 00:49:56.140 align:middle line:90%
This chart.

00:49:56.140 --> 00:49:58.960 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:49:58.960 --> 00:49:59.460 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:49:59.460 --> 00:50:02.513 align:middle line:84%
Now people keep
asking me about sounds

00:50:02.513 --> 00:50:03.930 align:middle line:84%
that I've been
carefully avoiding,

00:50:03.930 --> 00:50:05.970 align:middle line:90%
so let's talk about them.

00:50:05.970 --> 00:50:10.750 align:middle line:84%
There are what are
called approximants.

00:50:10.750 --> 00:50:14.140 align:middle line:84%
Approximants are not stops,
and they're not fricatives,

00:50:14.140 --> 00:50:15.550 align:middle line:90%
and they're not nasals.

00:50:15.550 --> 00:50:19.780 align:middle line:84%
They involve your
articulators vaguely

00:50:19.780 --> 00:50:24.250 align:middle line:84%
gesturing towards each other in
some part of your vocal tract,

00:50:24.250 --> 00:50:26.380 align:middle line:90%
not enough to make--

00:50:26.380 --> 00:50:28.660 align:middle line:84%
definitely not making contact,
and not enough to cause

00:50:28.660 --> 00:50:30.280 align:middle line:90%
any turbulence in the airflow.

00:50:30.280 --> 00:50:34.060 align:middle line:84%
So if you think about
a "w," let's say,

00:50:34.060 --> 00:50:38.328 align:middle line:84%
in the middle of a word like
"away," that's not a stop.

00:50:38.328 --> 00:50:39.370 align:middle line:90%
And it's not a fricative.

00:50:39.370 --> 00:50:40.240 align:middle line:90%
And it's not nasal.

00:50:40.240 --> 00:50:41.380 align:middle line:90%
It's bilabial.

00:50:41.380 --> 00:50:43.930 align:middle line:84%
You can feel your
lips engaging as you

00:50:43.930 --> 00:50:45.850 align:middle line:84%
make the "w" in the
middle of "away."

00:50:45.850 --> 00:50:47.470 align:middle line:90%
It's a bilabial sound.

00:50:47.470 --> 00:50:49.810 align:middle line:84%
But it's not a bilabial
stop or a fricative.

00:50:49.810 --> 00:50:52.970 align:middle line:84%
It is a bilabial
approximant, yeah?

00:50:52.970 --> 00:50:53.470 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:50:53.470 --> 00:50:57.550 align:middle line:84%
So similarly for the "y" sound
at the beginning of "year"

00:50:57.550 --> 00:51:03.550 align:middle line:84%
or "yard," and the "l" and the
"r" at the beginning and end

00:51:03.550 --> 00:51:07.990 align:middle line:84%
of, I don't know,
"layer" or "rail," yeah?

00:51:07.990 --> 00:51:11.320 align:middle line:84%
Those are all
approximants, yeah.

00:51:11.320 --> 00:51:16.330 align:middle line:84%
They are sometimes divided
into glides and liquids.

00:51:16.330 --> 00:51:18.760 align:middle line:84%
And I'm hoping that
nobody will ask me

00:51:18.760 --> 00:51:21.880 align:middle line:84%
how you know whether something
is a glide or a liquid.

00:51:21.880 --> 00:51:24.130 align:middle line:84%
Are you about to ask me how
you know whether something

00:51:24.130 --> 00:51:25.090 align:middle line:90%
is a glide or a liquid?

00:51:25.090 --> 00:51:26.630 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:51:26.630 --> 00:51:27.130 align:middle line:90%
Go ahead.

00:51:27.130 --> 00:51:27.520 align:middle line:90%
Is that what you're--

00:51:27.520 --> 00:51:28.510 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Well, yeah.

00:51:28.510 --> 00:51:29.552 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yes, OK.

00:51:29.552 --> 00:51:30.400 align:middle line:90%
All right, fine.

00:51:30.400 --> 00:51:33.070 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHS] So a glide--

00:51:33.070 --> 00:51:37.360 align:middle line:84%
sometimes, it's as though
a glide is an approximant

00:51:37.360 --> 00:51:41.140 align:middle line:84%
that if you were to hold it
longer, it would be a vowel.

00:51:41.140 --> 00:51:47.470 align:middle line:84%
So a "w"-- if you just
freeze yourself in "w" space,

00:51:47.470 --> 00:51:49.690 align:middle line:90%
you're making an "oo," right?

00:51:49.690 --> 00:51:52.365 align:middle line:90%
A "w" is like an "oo" sped up.

00:51:52.365 --> 00:51:53.990 align:middle line:84%
We're going to talk
about vowels later.

00:51:53.990 --> 00:51:56.500 align:middle line:84%
But if you think
about what an "oo" is,

00:51:56.500 --> 00:51:59.260 align:middle line:90%
it's a held version of a "w."

00:51:59.260 --> 00:52:04.570 align:middle line:84%
And similarly, a "y" sound,
yuh, is a sped-up version

00:52:04.570 --> 00:52:09.680 align:middle line:84%
of an "e" as opposed to an
"r" or an "l," which are just

00:52:09.680 --> 00:52:11.950 align:middle line:90%
something else.

00:52:11.950 --> 00:52:14.080 align:middle line:84%
Because I have not
given you a good way

00:52:14.080 --> 00:52:15.760 align:middle line:84%
of distinguishing
glides from liquids,

00:52:15.760 --> 00:52:17.860 align:middle line:84%
you can trust me
that I will never

00:52:17.860 --> 00:52:19.737 align:middle line:84%
ask you to distinguish
them in a way that

00:52:19.737 --> 00:52:22.070 align:middle line:84%
will make a difference for
grades or anything like that.

00:52:22.070 --> 00:52:23.890 align:middle line:84%
You will never see me
saying, no, you're wrong.

00:52:23.890 --> 00:52:24.723 align:middle line:90%
"That's not a glide.

00:52:24.723 --> 00:52:25.570 align:middle line:90%
It's a liquid."--

00:52:25.570 --> 00:52:26.200 align:middle line:90%
I may say that.

00:52:26.200 --> 00:52:30.610 align:middle line:84%
But there won't be
minus 5 next to it.

00:52:30.610 --> 00:52:32.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:52:32.230 --> 00:52:35.200 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: If the "w"
and the "y" sounds

00:52:35.200 --> 00:52:37.780 align:middle line:84%
are both just
shortened vowel sounds,

00:52:37.780 --> 00:52:42.083 align:middle line:84%
then why is it necessary to
have these as separate symbols?

00:52:42.083 --> 00:52:43.000 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

00:52:43.000 --> 00:52:45.010 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Just like
the "ch" having two--

00:52:45.010 --> 00:52:46.510 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Having two letters.

00:52:46.510 --> 00:52:47.760 align:middle line:90%
That's a really good question.

00:52:47.760 --> 00:52:51.200 align:middle line:84%
So why don't we just
use the letter "u"

00:52:51.200 --> 00:52:54.940 align:middle line:84%
for the bilabial
glide, for the "w"?

00:52:54.940 --> 00:53:00.102 align:middle line:84%
And let me see if I can come
up with a good answer to that.

00:53:00.102 --> 00:53:02.560 align:middle line:84%
Eventually, we're going to get
to-- so far, all we're doing

00:53:02.560 --> 00:53:05.470 align:middle line:84%
is talking about
sounds individually.

00:53:05.470 --> 00:53:07.360 align:middle line:84%
And eventually, we're
going to start talking

00:53:07.360 --> 00:53:10.390 align:middle line:90%
about sequences of sounds.

00:53:10.390 --> 00:53:14.740 align:middle line:84%
And we're going to want to be
able to talk about restrictions

00:53:14.740 --> 00:53:18.133 align:middle line:84%
on the ways in which sounds get
to interact with each other.

00:53:18.133 --> 00:53:20.050 align:middle line:84%
And so it's going to be
useful, at that point,

00:53:20.050 --> 00:53:22.060 align:middle line:84%
to be able to
distinguish, for example,

00:53:22.060 --> 00:53:24.760 align:middle line:90%
consonants from vowels.

00:53:24.760 --> 00:53:27.168 align:middle line:90%
And once we do that--

00:53:27.168 --> 00:53:28.960 align:middle line:84%
and you should feel
free to call me on this

00:53:28.960 --> 00:53:30.880 align:middle line:90%
if it doesn't come up later.

00:53:30.880 --> 00:53:33.050 align:middle line:90%
I'll try to make sure it does.

00:53:33.050 --> 00:53:34.750 align:middle line:84%
It'll turn out to
be useful to be

00:53:34.750 --> 00:53:38.680 align:middle line:84%
able to think of the consonant
version of this sound, the "w,"

00:53:38.680 --> 00:53:41.620 align:middle line:84%
and also the vowel version
of this sound, the "u."

00:53:41.620 --> 00:53:44.170 align:middle line:84%
But you're right that there
are plenty of sound changes

00:53:44.170 --> 00:53:47.172 align:middle line:84%
where you see them
converting back

00:53:47.172 --> 00:53:48.380 align:middle line:90%
and forth between each other.

00:53:48.380 --> 00:53:49.930 align:middle line:84%
That's a thing
that happens, yeah.

00:53:49.930 --> 00:53:52.570 align:middle line:90%
So yeah.

00:53:52.570 --> 00:53:55.090 align:middle line:84%
But that's the prima facie
reason for distinguishing them

00:53:55.090 --> 00:53:59.860 align:middle line:84%
because there are rules for
the distribution of sounds

00:53:59.860 --> 00:54:02.260 align:middle line:84%
for which it's useful to
have that distinction.

00:54:02.260 --> 00:54:03.700 align:middle line:84%
It's like anything
else, I guess.

00:54:03.700 --> 00:54:06.370 align:middle line:84%
We make that distinction
as long as it turns out

00:54:06.370 --> 00:54:08.080 align:middle line:84%
to be useful for
explaining stuff.

00:54:08.080 --> 00:54:09.670 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:54:09.670 --> 00:54:10.955 align:middle line:90%
Good question.

00:54:10.955 --> 00:54:11.455 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:54:11.455 --> 00:54:14.520 align:middle line:90%


00:54:14.520 --> 00:54:15.600 align:middle line:90%
Good, all right.

00:54:15.600 --> 00:54:18.780 align:middle line:90%


00:54:18.780 --> 00:54:20.400 align:middle line:84%
So those are glides
and liquids, OK.

00:54:20.400 --> 00:54:24.600 align:middle line:84%
And then I recall we were
asking about this before.

00:54:24.600 --> 00:54:27.000 align:middle line:90%
Sounds like chuh and juh--

00:54:27.000 --> 00:54:29.250 align:middle line:84%
I think I said you
could think of chuh

00:54:29.250 --> 00:54:32.740 align:middle line:84%
as a stop, a "t"
followed by a fricative,

00:54:32.740 --> 00:54:38.130 align:middle line:84%
shuh, or juh-- the
juh at the beginning

00:54:38.130 --> 00:54:41.820 align:middle line:84%
and end of a word
like judge as being

00:54:41.820 --> 00:54:46.980 align:middle line:84%
a stop, a "d" sound followed
by a fricative, a zhuh.

00:54:46.980 --> 00:54:49.897 align:middle line:84%
There's a little bit of a debate
about whether what I just said

00:54:49.897 --> 00:54:51.730 align:middle line:84%
is the right way to
think about this or not.

00:54:51.730 --> 00:54:54.040 align:middle line:84%
I think the standard way to
think about it is not exactly

00:54:54.040 --> 00:54:54.540 align:middle line:90%
that.

00:54:54.540 --> 00:54:57.040 align:middle line:84%
What people do is
say, yeah, there's

00:54:57.040 --> 00:54:59.690 align:middle line:90%
this package deal, an affricate.

00:54:59.690 --> 00:55:01.900 align:middle line:84%
So there's a name for
this kind of thing.

00:55:01.900 --> 00:55:07.180 align:middle line:84%
An affricate is this sequence,
something like a stop followed

00:55:07.180 --> 00:55:08.260 align:middle line:90%
by a fricative.

00:55:08.260 --> 00:55:11.317 align:middle line:84%
And the arguments
about whether or not

00:55:11.317 --> 00:55:13.150 align:middle line:84%
to make this move, about
whether to say, no,

00:55:13.150 --> 00:55:16.335 align:middle line:84%
that's a single sound
with a complex motion as

00:55:16.335 --> 00:55:17.710 align:middle line:84%
opposed to saying,
no, that's two

00:55:17.710 --> 00:55:19.967 align:middle line:84%
sounds right next
to each other has

00:55:19.967 --> 00:55:21.550 align:middle line:84%
to do with the kinds
of considerations

00:55:21.550 --> 00:55:22.540 align:middle line:90%
I was just talking about.

00:55:22.540 --> 00:55:23.957 align:middle line:84%
When you're trying
to figure out--

00:55:23.957 --> 00:55:25.780 align:middle line:84%
what's the best theory
of, for example,

00:55:25.780 --> 00:55:28.900 align:middle line:84%
what sequences of sounds
are allowed in a syllable

00:55:28.900 --> 00:55:30.700 align:middle line:90%
in a given language--

00:55:30.700 --> 00:55:33.700 align:middle line:84%
sometimes it's useful to be able
to say this is a language that

00:55:33.700 --> 00:55:37.150 align:middle line:84%
doesn't ever, ever allow,
say, a syllable to end

00:55:37.150 --> 00:55:39.280 align:middle line:84%
with a stop followed
by a fricative.

00:55:39.280 --> 00:55:41.680 align:middle line:84%
Oh, but it's OK for
it to end with chuh.

00:55:41.680 --> 00:55:43.960 align:middle line:84%
So we'll just treat that
as a single package, right?

00:55:43.960 --> 00:55:46.240 align:middle line:84%
We won't treat it as a stop
followed by a fricative.

00:55:46.240 --> 00:55:50.490 align:middle line:84%
That's the move
people make, yeah.

00:55:50.490 --> 00:55:53.870 align:middle line:90%
So terminology.

00:55:53.870 --> 00:55:54.370 align:middle line:90%
OK?

00:55:54.370 --> 00:55:57.630 align:middle line:90%


00:55:57.630 --> 00:55:59.480 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:55:59.480 --> 00:56:02.630 align:middle line:90%
So I've made the table whiter.

00:56:02.630 --> 00:56:05.660 align:middle line:84%
There are glides
like wuh and yuh.

00:56:05.660 --> 00:56:07.970 align:middle line:84%
There are some
other glides there

00:56:07.970 --> 00:56:09.470 align:middle line:90%
which I can try to read to you.

00:56:09.470 --> 00:56:12.290 align:middle line:84%
What would a labiodental
glide sound like?

00:56:12.290 --> 00:56:16.520 align:middle line:90%


00:56:16.520 --> 00:56:21.240 align:middle line:84%
What's a labiodental fricative,
a voiced labiodental fricative?

00:56:21.240 --> 00:56:22.140 align:middle line:90%
Vuh, right?

00:56:22.140 --> 00:56:23.800 align:middle line:90%
Like a "v" sound.

00:56:23.800 --> 00:56:27.180 align:middle line:84%
So can you try to make a
labiodental glide maybe

00:56:27.180 --> 00:56:28.410 align:middle line:90%
between vowels?

00:56:28.410 --> 00:56:30.720 align:middle line:84%
It's going to be something
like [NON-ENGLISH]..

00:56:30.720 --> 00:56:33.665 align:middle line:90%


00:56:33.665 --> 00:56:34.790 align:middle line:90%
English doesn't have those.

00:56:34.790 --> 00:56:36.050 align:middle line:90%
I believe Hindi does.

00:56:36.050 --> 00:56:39.050 align:middle line:84%
So there are languages out there
that have labiodental glides.

00:56:39.050 --> 00:56:43.760 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, there are velar
glides, [NON-ENGLISH],,

00:56:43.760 --> 00:56:47.210 align:middle line:84%
where your tongue is
just vaguely gesturing

00:56:47.210 --> 00:56:49.460 align:middle line:84%
in the direction of
your velum, yeah?

00:56:49.460 --> 00:56:52.010 align:middle line:84%
I like the IPA symbol
for the velar glide.

00:56:52.010 --> 00:56:55.190 align:middle line:84%
It looks like something
out of Tolkien.

00:56:55.190 --> 00:56:57.230 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:56:57.230 --> 00:57:00.110 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:57:00.110 --> 00:57:01.370 align:middle line:90%
All right.

00:57:01.370 --> 00:57:03.965 align:middle line:90%
So we've done consonants.

00:57:03.965 --> 00:57:05.780 align:middle line:84%
We have not done
all the consonants.

00:57:05.780 --> 00:57:08.162 align:middle line:84%
So what I'm going to do
is show you some vowels.

00:57:08.162 --> 00:57:09.620 align:middle line:84%
And then we'll
circle back and look

00:57:09.620 --> 00:57:11.480 align:middle line:84%
at some particularly
exotic consonants

00:57:11.480 --> 00:57:13.770 align:middle line:84%
probably next time,
probably not today.

00:57:13.770 --> 00:57:15.860 align:middle line:84%
So I want to start
talking about vowels.

00:57:15.860 --> 00:57:18.830 align:middle line:84%
So let's go through the
vowels systematically.

00:57:18.830 --> 00:57:21.830 align:middle line:84%
Compare the vowel in
the middle of bead

00:57:21.830 --> 00:57:24.180 align:middle line:84%
and the vowel in
the middle of bad.

00:57:24.180 --> 00:57:29.180 align:middle line:84%
And here let me just warn
you that the IPA becomes

00:57:29.180 --> 00:57:33.560 align:middle line:84%
particularly forbidding when
we get to vowels in English.

00:57:33.560 --> 00:57:35.780 align:middle line:84%
And it's not the
fault of the IPA.

00:57:35.780 --> 00:57:39.470 align:middle line:84%
It's the fact that English
has a very large number

00:57:39.470 --> 00:57:44.727 align:middle line:84%
of vowels and a not-very-good
system for writing them.

00:57:44.727 --> 00:57:47.060 align:middle line:84%
This is one of the things
that makes English spelling so

00:57:47.060 --> 00:57:49.880 align:middle line:84%
difficult that we can
actually have competitions

00:57:49.880 --> 00:57:51.680 align:middle line:90%
where you watch people spell.

00:57:51.680 --> 00:57:55.970 align:middle line:84%
This is a thing that in many
languages would be impossible.

00:57:55.970 --> 00:57:57.830 align:middle line:84%
If you tried to do
that in Finnish,

00:57:57.830 --> 00:57:59.870 align:middle line:84%
the spelling bee
would just never end

00:57:59.870 --> 00:58:03.800 align:middle line:84%
because every word is pronounced
exactly the way it's spelled.

00:58:03.800 --> 00:58:05.720 align:middle line:90%
We don't do that in English.

00:58:05.720 --> 00:58:07.940 align:middle line:90%
So here are two vowels.

00:58:07.940 --> 00:58:09.950 align:middle line:90%
And those are their IPA symbols.

00:58:09.950 --> 00:58:12.590 align:middle line:84%
The vowel in "bead" and
the vowel in "bad"--

00:58:12.590 --> 00:58:17.840 align:middle line:84%
and now so everybody joined
me in transitioning from one

00:58:17.840 --> 00:58:19.130 align:middle line:90%
of those vowels to the other.

00:58:19.130 --> 00:58:29.770 align:middle line:90%
Go ee-ah, ee-ah, ee-ah, OK.

00:58:29.770 --> 00:58:31.220 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

00:58:31.220 --> 00:58:33.320 align:middle line:90%
You guys sound good.

00:58:33.320 --> 00:58:35.695 align:middle line:90%
What are you doing?

00:58:35.695 --> 00:58:37.070 align:middle line:84%
You're doing what
I asked you to,

00:58:37.070 --> 00:58:40.160 align:middle line:84%
but what's happening
in your mouth?

00:58:40.160 --> 00:58:41.203 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

00:58:41.203 --> 00:58:42.620 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: The second
vowel is open.

00:58:42.620 --> 00:58:44.180 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, the
second level is more open.

00:58:44.180 --> 00:58:46.400 align:middle line:84%
You're opening your
mouth a little bit more.

00:58:46.400 --> 00:58:49.403 align:middle line:84%
What are you doing
with your tongue?

00:58:49.403 --> 00:58:51.320 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Releasing from
the top of your mouth.

00:58:51.320 --> 00:58:52.490 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, it's lowering.

00:58:52.490 --> 00:58:53.720 align:middle line:84%
It's going from the
top to the bottom.

00:58:53.720 --> 00:58:55.303 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, I think you
guys are both right.

00:58:55.303 --> 00:58:57.898 align:middle line:84%
So for "ee," your
tongue is tense.

00:58:57.898 --> 00:58:59.690 align:middle line:84%
And it's up there at
the top of your mouth.

00:58:59.690 --> 00:59:03.267 align:middle line:84%
And then for "ah," your
tongue drops, right?

00:59:03.267 --> 00:59:05.600 align:middle line:84%
And in fact, it drops so far
that it drags your jaw down

00:59:05.600 --> 00:59:06.680 align:middle line:90%
with it, right?

00:59:06.680 --> 00:59:08.750 align:middle line:84%
Maybe there's a more
reasonable way to say that.

00:59:08.750 --> 00:59:10.430 align:middle line:84%
You lower your jaw
so that your tongue

00:59:10.430 --> 00:59:12.410 align:middle line:90%
can go even further down.

00:59:12.410 --> 00:59:14.960 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

00:59:14.960 --> 00:59:20.640 align:middle line:84%
So one way of classifying
vowels is in terms of height.

00:59:20.640 --> 00:59:24.740 align:middle line:84%
So we talk about the high vowel,
like "heat," and the low vowel,

00:59:24.740 --> 00:59:25.970 align:middle line:90%
like "hat."

00:59:25.970 --> 00:59:29.300 align:middle line:84%
And there are vowels in-between,
like the vowel in "hate."

00:59:29.300 --> 00:59:31.130 align:middle line:90%
That vowel is called mid.

00:59:31.130 --> 00:59:33.053 align:middle line:84%
So we have high vowels
and we have mid vowels.

00:59:33.053 --> 00:59:34.220 align:middle line:90%
And then we have low vowels.

00:59:34.220 --> 00:59:39.950 align:middle line:90%


00:59:39.950 --> 00:59:42.470 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

00:59:42.470 --> 00:59:43.070 align:middle line:90%
OK.

00:59:43.070 --> 00:59:46.080 align:middle line:90%
Now let's do another comparison.

00:59:46.080 --> 00:59:50.150 align:middle line:84%
Think about the vowel in
"he" and the vowel in "who."

00:59:50.150 --> 00:59:59.900 align:middle line:84%
So everybody go ee,
ooh, ee, ooh, ee, ooh.

00:59:59.900 --> 01:00:03.050 align:middle line:84%
What's going on in your
vocal tract as you do that?

01:00:03.050 --> 01:00:09.720 align:middle line:90%


01:00:09.720 --> 01:00:12.424 align:middle line:84%
What's the difference
between "ee" and "ooh"?

01:00:12.424 --> 01:00:13.320 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Your lips?

01:00:13.320 --> 01:00:13.830 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Your lips.

01:00:13.830 --> 01:00:15.390 align:middle line:84%
Your lips are
definitely involved.

01:00:15.390 --> 01:00:15.990 align:middle line:90%
Yes.

01:00:15.990 --> 01:00:18.990 align:middle line:84%
So for "ooh," they're
like this, right?

01:00:18.990 --> 01:00:20.760 align:middle line:90%
Your lips are rounded.

01:00:20.760 --> 01:00:22.740 align:middle line:84%
For the "ooh," that's
absolutely right.

01:00:22.740 --> 01:00:23.490 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:00:23.490 --> 01:00:25.500 align:middle line:84%
And then for
"heat," they're not.

01:00:25.500 --> 01:00:26.010 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:00:26.010 --> 01:00:28.980 align:middle line:84%
In fact, that's why when you
take photographs of people,

01:00:28.980 --> 01:00:31.530 align:middle line:84%
you have them say something
with an "ee" vowel in it,

01:00:31.530 --> 01:00:35.637 align:middle line:84%
like "cheese," just to get
them to not round their lips.

01:00:35.637 --> 01:00:37.470 align:middle line:84%
But you're doing something
with your tongue,

01:00:37.470 --> 01:00:43.520 align:middle line:84%
too, as you go from
"ee" to "ooh," ee, ooh.

01:00:43.520 --> 01:00:46.320 align:middle line:84%
What are you doing
with your tongue?

01:00:46.320 --> 01:00:46.820 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:00:46.820 --> 01:00:49.035 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Moving it
forward [INAUDIBLE]??

01:00:49.035 --> 01:00:49.910 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Wow.

01:00:49.910 --> 01:00:53.570 align:middle line:90%
So let's start with "ee."

01:00:53.570 --> 01:00:56.150 align:middle line:90%
Where is your tongue?

01:00:56.150 --> 01:00:59.980 align:middle line:84%
It's in your mouth, but
where is it pointing?

01:00:59.980 --> 01:01:01.360 align:middle line:90%
It's high, right?

01:01:01.360 --> 01:01:02.770 align:middle line:90%
"Ee."

01:01:02.770 --> 01:01:06.360 align:middle line:84%
And then for "ooh,"
where does it go?

01:01:06.360 --> 01:01:11.390 align:middle line:90%


01:01:11.390 --> 01:01:12.970 align:middle line:90%
It moves, right?

01:01:12.970 --> 01:01:14.560 align:middle line:84%
So you aren't just
rounding your lips

01:01:14.560 --> 01:01:15.760 align:middle line:84%
and leaving your
tongue where it was.

01:01:15.760 --> 01:01:16.450 align:middle line:90%
Yes?

01:01:16.450 --> 01:01:18.070 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I noticed
for "ee," my tongue

01:01:18.070 --> 01:01:19.450 align:middle line:90%
is also between my molars.

01:01:19.450 --> 01:01:21.136 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:01:21.136 --> 01:01:22.077 align:middle line:90%
Ah, yeah.

01:01:22.077 --> 01:01:22.910 align:middle line:90%
I see what you mean.

01:01:22.910 --> 01:01:24.643 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yeah.

01:01:24.643 --> 01:01:25.810 align:middle line:90%
I don't know about you guys.

01:01:25.810 --> 01:01:27.340 align:middle line:90%
But for me, for "ee"--

01:01:27.340 --> 01:01:28.870 align:middle line:90%
yeah?

01:01:28.870 --> 01:01:31.930 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, for me, for "ee,"
it's at the front.

01:01:31.930 --> 01:01:32.860 align:middle line:90%
And it's high.

01:01:32.860 --> 01:01:37.090 align:middle line:84%
And then for "ooh," my
tongue curls backward.

01:01:37.090 --> 01:01:38.290 align:middle line:90%
And it avoids my molars.

01:01:38.290 --> 01:01:39.722 align:middle line:90%
You're absolutely right.

01:01:39.722 --> 01:01:41.680 align:middle line:84%
And it curls backwards
so that it's hiding back

01:01:41.680 --> 01:01:42.680 align:middle line:90%
in the back of my mouth.

01:01:42.680 --> 01:01:44.890 align:middle line:84%
I think that's the thing
you were saying just now.

01:01:44.890 --> 01:01:45.520 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:01:45.520 --> 01:01:47.228 align:middle line:84%
Is that the experience
people are having?

01:01:47.228 --> 01:01:48.905 align:middle line:84%
Everybody do some
more ee, ooh, ee, ooh.

01:01:48.905 --> 01:01:50.530 align:middle line:84%
Feel your tongue
moving back and forth.

01:01:50.530 --> 01:01:51.670 align:middle line:90%
Ignore your lips, right?

01:01:51.670 --> 01:01:53.266 align:middle line:90%
And think about your tongue.

01:01:53.266 --> 01:01:55.700 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

01:01:55.700 --> 01:01:57.056 align:middle line:90%
Yep.

01:01:57.056 --> 01:02:03.070 align:middle line:84%
OK, so we have high,
mid, and low vowels.

01:02:03.070 --> 01:02:06.010 align:middle line:84%
But we also have
front and back vowels.

01:02:06.010 --> 01:02:08.830 align:middle line:84%
So the back vowels are vowels
like the vowel in "who"

01:02:08.830 --> 01:02:12.460 align:middle line:84%
and the vowel in "hoed," and
the vowel in "hot," "ah."

01:02:12.460 --> 01:02:16.120 align:middle line:84%
So "ooh" and "oh" and "ah"--
those are all back vowels.

01:02:16.120 --> 01:02:19.710 align:middle line:84%
Your tongue is pulling back
toward the back of your mouth.

01:02:19.710 --> 01:02:21.290 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:02:21.290 --> 01:02:21.790 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:02:21.790 --> 01:02:25.450 align:middle line:84%
And then this is the other
point that you guys made.

01:02:25.450 --> 01:02:27.080 align:middle line:84%
Some of these
vowels are rounded.

01:02:27.080 --> 01:02:31.780 align:middle line:84%
So in English, the
back non-low vowels,

01:02:31.780 --> 01:02:35.530 align:middle line:84%
the back high and the back
mid vowels, are rounded in who

01:02:35.530 --> 01:02:36.820 align:middle line:90%
and hoe.

01:02:36.820 --> 01:02:39.760 align:middle line:84%
Your lips have to round
to make those vowels.

01:02:39.760 --> 01:02:42.950 align:middle line:84%
But for hot, your
lips don't round.

01:02:42.950 --> 01:02:44.190 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:02:44.190 --> 01:02:46.195 align:middle line:90%
And a bunch more symbols.

01:02:46.195 --> 01:02:47.320 align:middle line:90%
Some of them aren't so bad.

01:02:47.320 --> 01:02:49.470 align:middle line:84%
The letter "u" for "ooh"--
that's pretty good.

01:02:49.470 --> 01:02:51.850 align:middle line:84%
And the letter "o"
for "oh," yeah.

01:02:51.850 --> 01:02:54.860 align:middle line:84%
And then we have that
ash symbol there,

01:02:54.860 --> 01:02:58.470 align:middle line:84%
which is from Old English
for "ah" in "had."

01:02:58.470 --> 01:03:01.670 align:middle line:84%
And then "i" and "e" and
"a," stuck with those.

01:03:01.670 --> 01:03:02.460 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:03:02.460 --> 01:03:05.780 align:middle line:84%
We have standard-issue
European values

01:03:05.780 --> 01:03:11.020 align:middle line:90%
for some of those symptoms, OK?

01:03:11.020 --> 01:03:21.420 align:middle line:84%
Now in this chart, I've
only got six vowels.

01:03:21.420 --> 01:03:23.170 align:middle line:84%
You may have been
taught-- how many vowels

01:03:23.170 --> 01:03:27.070 align:middle line:84%
were you taught English had if
you went to school in English?

01:03:27.070 --> 01:03:27.912 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:03:27.912 --> 01:03:28.495 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Five.

01:03:28.495 --> 01:03:28.780 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Five.

01:03:28.780 --> 01:03:30.130 align:middle line:84%
It's supposed to
have five, right?

01:03:30.130 --> 01:03:31.090 align:middle line:90%
It does not have five.

01:03:31.090 --> 01:03:33.490 align:middle line:90%
It has 14.

01:03:33.490 --> 01:03:37.615 align:middle line:84%
And why are we taught
that it only has five?

01:03:37.615 --> 01:03:40.375 align:middle line:84%
Why do we only have
five letters for vowels?

01:03:40.375 --> 01:03:43.400 align:middle line:90%


01:03:43.400 --> 01:03:46.040 align:middle line:90%
Who gave us this alphabet?

01:03:46.040 --> 01:03:47.660 align:middle line:90%
The Romans, right?

01:03:47.660 --> 01:03:48.260 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:03:48.260 --> 01:03:50.870 align:middle line:84%
And in Latin, there in
fact are five vowels, which

01:03:50.870 --> 01:03:52.500 align:middle line:90%
can be either long or short.

01:03:52.500 --> 01:03:54.980 align:middle line:84%
So this is a perfect
alphabet for Latin, yeah?

01:03:54.980 --> 01:03:56.970 align:middle line:90%
But then we got hold of it.

01:03:56.970 --> 01:04:01.310 align:middle line:84%
And so we've ended up
with 12, 14 vowels.

01:04:01.310 --> 01:04:03.290 align:middle line:84%
Different dialects of
English are different.

01:04:03.290 --> 01:04:05.570 align:middle line:84%
And we have five letters
to spell them with.

01:04:05.570 --> 01:04:08.540 align:middle line:84%
And this is why we have
spelling bees, yeah--

01:04:08.540 --> 01:04:09.970 align:middle line:90%
one of the reasons.

01:04:09.970 --> 01:04:15.120 align:middle line:84%
OK, so I've written six of our
five vowels here on this chart.

01:04:15.120 --> 01:04:17.820 align:middle line:90%
And then we have more.

01:04:17.820 --> 01:04:22.430 align:middle line:84%
So think about "ooh"
and "uh," in "who'd"

01:04:22.430 --> 01:04:27.680 align:middle line:84%
and "hood," or "ee" and
"ih" in "heed" and "hid,"

01:04:27.680 --> 01:04:31.220 align:middle line:84%
or "ay" and "eh" in
"raid" and "red,"

01:04:31.220 --> 01:04:36.470 align:middle line:84%
or "oh" and "aw" like
in "coat" and "caught."

01:04:36.470 --> 01:04:38.960 align:middle line:84%
There are various ways of
talking about this distinction.

01:04:38.960 --> 01:04:41.065 align:middle line:90%
But one way is like this.

01:04:41.065 --> 01:04:42.440 align:middle line:84%
We say that there's
a distinction

01:04:42.440 --> 01:04:44.090 align:middle line:84%
between what are
called tense vowels

01:04:44.090 --> 01:04:46.640 align:middle line:90%
and what are called lax vowels.

01:04:46.640 --> 01:04:52.760 align:middle line:84%
So "ooh" is a high back
rounded tense vowel.

01:04:52.760 --> 01:04:56.063 align:middle line:84%
And if you go back and forth
between "ooh" and "uh,"

01:04:56.063 --> 01:05:01.410 align:middle line:84%
ooh, uh, ooh, uh, ooh,
uh, the idea is basically

01:05:01.410 --> 01:05:03.570 align:middle line:90%
what you're doing is relaxing.

01:05:03.570 --> 01:05:07.560 align:middle line:84%
So your tongue is not quite
as rigidly high and back

01:05:07.560 --> 01:05:10.290 align:middle line:84%
as it would be for an "ooh"
when it makes an "uh."

01:05:10.290 --> 01:05:13.030 align:middle line:84%
And your lips are probably
not quite as tensely rounded,

01:05:13.030 --> 01:05:13.530 align:middle line:90%
either.

01:05:13.530 --> 01:05:16.560 align:middle line:90%
Your whole body relaxes, yeah?

01:05:16.560 --> 01:05:20.010 align:middle line:84%
Same deal with all
these other pairs.

01:05:20.010 --> 01:05:22.410 align:middle line:84%
It's a fact about
English lax vowels

01:05:22.410 --> 01:05:25.860 align:middle line:84%
that there's a restriction
on their distribution.

01:05:25.860 --> 01:05:29.610 align:middle line:84%
English monosyllables
don't end in lax vowels

01:05:29.610 --> 01:05:30.970 align:middle line:90%
that are either front or high.

01:05:30.970 --> 01:05:35.190 align:middle line:84%
So we have words like
"flee" and "flu" and "flay."

01:05:35.190 --> 01:05:37.620 align:middle line:90%
Those are all words in English.

01:05:37.620 --> 01:05:40.990 align:middle line:84%
"Flay" means to
remove the skin from.

01:05:40.990 --> 01:05:44.310 align:middle line:84%
But we don't have those
three words at the end there.

01:05:44.310 --> 01:05:48.000 align:middle line:84%
I've given them stars to remind
us that those are not words

01:05:48.000 --> 01:05:49.590 align:middle line:90%
that we could have in English.

01:05:49.590 --> 01:05:53.340 align:middle line:84%
Anybody want to attempt
to pronounce them?

01:05:53.340 --> 01:05:54.340 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:05:54.340 --> 01:05:54.740 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Wait, which ones--

01:05:54.740 --> 01:05:55.650 align:middle line:90%
I was going to ask a question.

01:05:55.650 --> 01:05:56.160 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Oh, sorry.

01:05:56.160 --> 01:05:56.850 align:middle line:84%
You were going to
ask a question.

01:05:56.850 --> 01:05:57.600 align:middle line:90%
Go ahead.

01:05:57.600 --> 01:05:59.070 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: What about schwa?

01:05:59.070 --> 01:06:00.190 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: We
haven't gotten to schwa.

01:06:00.190 --> 01:06:00.570 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: OK.

01:06:00.570 --> 01:06:00.780 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah, yeah.

01:06:00.780 --> 01:06:01.500 align:middle line:90%
There are more vowels.

01:06:01.500 --> 01:06:02.000 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:06:02.000 --> 01:06:05.220 align:middle line:90%


01:06:05.220 --> 01:06:07.960 align:middle line:84%
Those last three-- if
they were English words,

01:06:07.960 --> 01:06:10.620 align:middle line:84%
they would be "flih,"
"fluh," and "fleh," right?

01:06:10.620 --> 01:06:12.180 align:middle line:84%
Well, we don't have
words like that.

01:06:12.180 --> 01:06:17.115 align:middle line:84%
English doesn't have words that
end in "ih," or "uh," or "eh,"

01:06:17.115 --> 01:06:20.620 align:middle line:84%
with the possible
exception of "meh."

01:06:20.620 --> 01:06:23.160 align:middle line:84%
So if we don't count
that as a word,

01:06:23.160 --> 01:06:24.870 align:middle line:84%
then English doesn't
have monosyllables

01:06:24.870 --> 01:06:26.663 align:middle line:90%
that end in these vowels, yeah.

01:06:26.663 --> 01:06:27.450 align:middle line:90%
OK.

01:06:27.450 --> 01:06:32.235 align:middle line:84%
And then you wanted
to know about schwa.

01:06:32.235 --> 01:06:33.985 align:middle line:84%
Those are sometimes
called central vowels.

01:06:33.985 --> 01:06:36.940 align:middle line:90%


01:06:36.940 --> 01:06:39.730 align:middle line:84%
Sorry, the first vowel
in "machine" is a schwa.

01:06:39.730 --> 01:06:40.900 align:middle line:90%
It's not higher back.

01:06:40.900 --> 01:06:43.210 align:middle line:90%
It's not front.

01:06:43.210 --> 01:06:44.680 align:middle line:90%
It's not high or low.

01:06:44.680 --> 01:06:46.240 align:middle line:90%
It's mid and central.

01:06:46.240 --> 01:06:49.960 align:middle line:84%
And then "dove" [the bird]
is pretty similar to schwa

01:06:49.960 --> 01:06:52.520 align:middle line:84%
and is sometimes represented
with that wedge shape there.

01:06:52.520 --> 01:06:53.920 align:middle line:90%
In fact, it's called a wedge.

01:06:53.920 --> 01:06:57.430 align:middle line:84%
Not all speakers of English
distinguish schwa from wedge.

01:06:57.430 --> 01:06:58.100 align:middle line:90%
I do.

01:06:58.100 --> 01:07:01.480 align:middle line:84%
So for me, the vowels in above
are two different vowels--

01:07:01.480 --> 01:07:02.590 align:middle line:90%
above.

01:07:02.590 --> 01:07:04.840 align:middle line:84%
But there are
speakers of English

01:07:04.840 --> 01:07:07.380 align:middle line:90%
for whom they're the same.

01:07:07.380 --> 01:07:11.610 align:middle line:84%
Similarly, let me go
back to an earlier slide,

01:07:11.610 --> 01:07:14.370 align:middle line:84%
not every dialect of English
has all of these vowels

01:07:14.370 --> 01:07:16.360 align:middle line:84%
or has them all in
the same places.

01:07:16.360 --> 01:07:20.610 align:middle line:84%
So I speak a dialect of English
in which "caught" and "cot" are

01:07:20.610 --> 01:07:22.930 align:middle line:84%
different, have
different vowels in them.

01:07:22.930 --> 01:07:25.200 align:middle line:84%
Does anybody pronounce
those words the same?

01:07:25.200 --> 01:07:26.292 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:07:26.292 --> 01:07:27.750 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: I pronounce
them the same.

01:07:27.750 --> 01:07:28.667 align:middle line:90%
But I have a question.

01:07:28.667 --> 01:07:29.990 align:middle line:90%
You said that they can't--

01:07:29.990 --> 01:07:33.250 align:middle line:84%
English monosyllables
can't end in lax vowels.

01:07:33.250 --> 01:07:34.450 align:middle line:90%
What about "law"?

01:07:34.450 --> 01:07:35.820 align:middle line:90%
I know that ends in--

01:07:35.820 --> 01:07:36.737 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:07:36.737 --> 01:07:38.640 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

01:07:38.640 --> 01:07:40.620 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
Yeah, yeah, yeah.

01:07:40.620 --> 01:07:41.700 align:middle line:90%
So what did I say?

01:07:41.700 --> 01:07:45.060 align:middle line:84%
They can't end in lax vowels
that are either front or high.

01:07:45.060 --> 01:07:46.390 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Oh.

01:07:46.390 --> 01:07:49.330 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: So yeah,
you're absolutely right.

01:07:49.330 --> 01:07:51.300 align:middle line:84%
There are ones ending
in "aw," like "law."

01:07:51.300 --> 01:07:52.770 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, absolutely.

01:07:52.770 --> 01:07:55.440 align:middle line:90%
Or "flaw."

01:07:55.440 --> 01:07:58.315 align:middle line:84%
There are people who pronounce
"caught" and "cot" the same.

01:07:58.315 --> 01:07:59.940 align:middle line:84%
For me, those are
two different vowels.

01:07:59.940 --> 01:08:01.260 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, can you
pronounce them for us?

01:08:01.260 --> 01:08:02.200 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: "Caught" and "cot."
[pronounced identically]

01:08:02.200 --> 01:08:03.510 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: OK, cool.

01:08:03.510 --> 01:08:06.327 align:middle line:84%
Did you say them in the same
order or a different order?

01:08:06.327 --> 01:08:07.410 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: It didn't matter.

01:08:07.410 --> 01:08:07.950 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS:
It doesn't matter.

01:08:07.950 --> 01:08:08.490 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

01:08:08.490 --> 01:08:10.837 align:middle line:90%
[LAUGHTER]

01:08:10.837 --> 01:08:13.890 align:middle line:90%
I pronounce those three words--

01:08:13.890 --> 01:08:15.780 align:middle line:90%
"merry," "marry," and "Mary"--

01:08:15.780 --> 01:08:17.123 align:middle line:90%
I pronounce them the same.

01:08:17.123 --> 01:08:18.540 align:middle line:84%
There are dialects
of English that

01:08:18.540 --> 01:08:20.830 align:middle line:84%
have different pronunciations
for some of them.

01:08:20.830 --> 01:08:24.420 align:middle line:84%
Does anybody pronounce
these words differently?

01:08:24.420 --> 01:08:25.529 align:middle line:90%
Yeah?

01:08:25.529 --> 01:08:27.180 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: "Merry,"
"marry," and "Mary."

01:08:27.180 --> 01:08:28.347 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Mary, yeah.

01:08:28.347 --> 01:08:31.080 align:middle line:84%
So there are people for whom
the vowel in the last one

01:08:31.080 --> 01:08:32.069 align:middle line:90%
is more of an ash.

01:08:32.069 --> 01:08:33.660 align:middle line:90%
It's like a "Mary."

01:08:33.660 --> 01:08:37.470 align:middle line:84%
I associate that with
New Jersey, where--

01:08:37.470 --> 01:08:38.690 align:middle line:90%
you're from New Jersey.

01:08:38.690 --> 01:08:40.740 align:middle line:90%
Yeah, yes.

01:08:40.740 --> 01:08:43.560 align:middle line:90%
I'll pay you later.

01:08:43.560 --> 01:08:44.850 align:middle line:90%
OK, yeah.

01:08:44.850 --> 01:08:48.930 align:middle line:84%
So OK, we've now
rocketed through a bunch

01:08:48.930 --> 01:08:50.332 align:middle line:90%
of consonants and vowels.

01:08:50.332 --> 01:08:51.540 align:middle line:90%
We're just about out of time.

01:08:51.540 --> 01:08:53.946 align:middle line:84%
Let me just give this
to you as an exercise.

01:08:53.946 --> 01:08:54.779 align:middle line:90%
And then we'll stop.

01:08:54.779 --> 01:08:56.946 align:middle line:84%
Anybody want to try to
pronounce the first of those?

01:08:56.946 --> 01:09:01.211 align:middle line:90%


01:09:01.211 --> 01:09:03.401 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: Sells.

01:09:03.401 --> 01:09:04.859 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: Oh, "She
sells seashells."

01:09:04.859 --> 01:09:06.510 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "She
sells seashells."

01:09:06.510 --> 01:09:08.649 align:middle line:90%
Yeah.

01:09:08.649 --> 01:09:09.580 align:middle line:90%
I'll tell you what.

01:09:09.580 --> 01:09:11.240 align:middle line:84%
We'll do this
exercise next time.

01:09:11.240 --> 01:09:14.680 align:middle line:84%
So next time, we'll do
this as the first slide.

01:09:14.680 --> 01:09:16.359 align:middle line:90%
And I'll leave it on the slides.

01:09:16.359 --> 01:09:19.343 align:middle line:84%
You can work on it at home
and do some fooling with it

01:09:19.343 --> 01:09:21.010 align:middle line:84%
and try to get to
where you can read it.

01:09:21.010 --> 01:09:22.990 align:middle line:84%
As we go along, I'm
going to be asking

01:09:22.990 --> 01:09:24.220 align:middle line:90%
you to read things in IPA.

01:09:24.220 --> 01:09:26.500 align:middle line:84%
So I'll start putting IPA
on the slides more and more.

01:09:26.500 --> 01:09:28.630 align:middle line:84%
So start trying to
familiarize yourself with it

01:09:28.630 --> 01:09:30.850 align:middle line:84%
and get to where you're
familiar with at least

01:09:30.850 --> 01:09:33.713 align:middle line:84%
the symbols for sounds
that we use in English.

01:09:33.713 --> 01:09:35.380 align:middle line:84%
Do you want me to
read the rest of them?

01:09:35.380 --> 01:09:36.310 align:middle line:90%
I'll do some more IPA.

01:09:36.310 --> 01:09:38.703 align:middle line:90%
OK, so what's the second one?

01:09:38.703 --> 01:09:40.161 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: "Sue says
he's a bad egg."

01:09:40.161 --> 01:09:41.953 align:middle line:84%
NORVIN RICHARDS: "Sue
says he's a bad egg."

01:09:41.953 --> 01:09:43.510 align:middle line:84%
Yeah, you guys
are fast learners.

01:09:43.510 --> 01:09:45.285 align:middle line:90%
And what's the third one?

01:09:45.285 --> 01:09:46.375 align:middle line:90%
STUDENT: [INAUDIBLE]

01:09:46.375 --> 01:09:47.500 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Excellent.

01:09:47.500 --> 01:09:49.771 align:middle line:90%
And what's the last one?

01:09:49.771 --> 01:09:53.933 align:middle line:84%
STUDENT: "Top chopstick
shops stock top chopsticks."

01:09:53.933 --> 01:09:54.850 align:middle line:90%
NORVIN RICHARDS: Yeah.

01:09:54.850 --> 01:09:58.720 align:middle line:84%
"Top chopstick shops
stock top chopsticks."

01:09:58.720 --> 01:10:01.060 align:middle line:84%
Got that from a book
of tongue twisters.

01:10:01.060 --> 01:10:01.720 align:middle line:90%
OK, good.

01:10:01.720 --> 01:10:02.860 align:middle line:90%
So let's leave it there.

01:10:02.860 --> 01:10:06.480 align:middle line:84%
And we'll come back
to this next time.

01:10:06.480 --> 01:10:11.000 align:middle line:90%